=begin
  itemdb.lic -- catalog your enhancive and magic items into a searchable database.

  Reads the loresong off each item in a container (and ANALYZEs any book or codex, whose
  spell-specialization detail the loresong never shows), stores what it finds in a YAML
  database, and lets you search that database by enhancive, flares, worn slot, magic spell,
  and more.  Run
  `;itemdb` with no arguments for the full command and query reference.

  Examples:
    ;itemdb scan backpack           read every new item in your backpack into the database
    ;itemdb neck & (dex | ranged)   find neckworn items that boost Dexterity or Ranged

  CHANGELOG
    1.7.0  catalog spell-specialization books: an item that looks like a book/codex is ANALYZEd
           (its loresong shows nothing useful), recording which spell it specializes and how
           many specialization ranks READing it grants; search with spec, spec:<spell|number>,
           specranks>=N
    1.6.1  get/put items on surface furniture (armor stand, weapon rack, ...) with "on", not
           "in" -- putting a piece back "in" an armor stand failed and left it in hand
    1.6.0  track sighting and the padding/weighting degree (with CER band + service cost),
           ensorcell/sanctify tiers, max enchant, and last enchanter; a permanent Holy Fire
           flare now reads as sanctify tier 6; track searchable damage resistances
           (resist:<type>[>N]); parse graded treatments ("very heavily padded") and
           "an"-class complexity ("an intricate project")
    1.5.0  grouped, nestable boolean queries -- e.g. neck & (dex | ranged)
    1.4.0  single-ear and both-ears worn slots kept apart (ear / 2ear)
    1.3.0  worn-slot limits read from INVENTORY USAGE; totals measured against them
    1.2.0  entries re-keyed by item name (login item ids don't persist across sessions)
    1.0.0  initial release -- scan containers, parse loresongs, YAML database, keyword search

  author: Drafix
  game: gs
  tags: utility, enhancive, inventory
  required: Lich >= 5.0.0
  version: 1.7.0
=end

require 'yaml'

module ItemDB
  VERSION = '1.7.0'

  # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- taxonomy

  # The ten GemStone stats.  Stats are the only things that boost "Base"; everything with
  # a Bonus/Ranks suffix that isn't one of these is a skill, and everything with no suffix
  # at all (Max Mana, Stamina Recovery, ...) is a resource.
  STAT_NAMES = %w[Strength Constitution Dexterity Agility Discipline
                  Aura Logic Intuition Wisdom Influence].freeze

  STAT_CAP  = 40  # in base-equivalent points; 1 point of stat bonus == 2 base
  SKILL_CAP = 50

  BOOST_TYPES = %w[base bonus ranks].freeze

  # Enhancive charges are reported as a vague phrase unless the loresong is skilled enough,
  # in which case you get the real number (which happens below 10).  phrase => [tier, min,
  # max]; max nil means open ended.
  #
  #   confirmed: 1-9 exact, "quite a few" 10-19, "several" 20-29, 30+ is the vague top end.
  #   estimated: how the four 30+ phrases below divide that top end.  Tune them freely --
  #              only these four are guesses, and nothing else in the script depends on the
  #              exact numbers (charge comparisons match on range overlap).
  CHARGE_TIERS = {
    'no'                                          => [0, 0, 0],
    'one'                                         => [1, 1, 1],
    'a couple of'                                 => [2, 2, 3],
    'a few'                                       => [2, 2, 9],
    'quite a few'                                 => [3, 10, 19],
    'several'                                     => [4, 20, 29],
    'a fair amount of'                            => [5, 30, 49],
    'a lot of'                                    => [6, 50, 99],
    'a huge number of'                            => [7, 100, 249],
    'more than your average giantman could count' => [8, 250, nil]
  }.freeze

  # Loresong flare messages => the flare they actually mean.  Matched in order against the
  # distinctive tail of the line, so both phrasings ("It has been infused with ..." and
  # "The harmonics generated tell you that the <item> has been infused with ...") land on
  # the same entry, and "temporarily" only decides the temporary flag.
  # NOTE: guiding light must be tested before plasma -- "divine flaming substance" would
  # otherwise be swallowed by the plain "flaming substance" pattern.
  FLARE_MESSAGES = [
    [/corrosive substance/i,                                 'acid'],
    [/occasionally focus their magical prowess/i,             'acuity'],
    [/elemental air|\ban air elemental/i,                     'air'],
    [/an ice elemental|elemental cold/i,                      'cold'],
    [/disintegrating substance/i,                             'disintegration'],
    [/strange anti-magical substance/i,                       'dispel'],
    [/disrupting substance/i,                                 'disruption'],
    [/an earth elemental|elemental earth/i,                   'earth'],
    [/a fire elemental|elemental fire/i,                      'fire'],
    [/inflicts more fearsome wounds when it strikes/i,        'firewheel'],
    [/grappling force/i,                                      'grapple'],
    [/divine flaming substance/i,                             'guiding light'],
    [/a lightning bolt|infused with lightning/i,              'lightning'],
    [/molten substance/i,                                     'magma'],
    [/infused with mana/i,                                    'mana'],
    [/flaming substance/i,                                    'plasma'],
    [/steaming substance/i,                                   'steam'],
    [/Protects against magical attacks/i,                     'td'],
    [/substance of pure terror/i,                             'terror'],
    [/dark substance/i,                                       'vacuum'],
    [/a water elemental/i,                                    'water'],
    [/infused with death/i,                                   'xazkruvrixis']
  ].freeze

  # Gerund the game uses => the verb you'd actually type.
  ACTIVATORS = {
    'waving' => 'wave', 'tapping' => 'tap', 'rubbing' => 'rub', 'turning' => 'turn',
    'pushing' => 'push', 'pulling' => 'pull', 'pointing' => 'point', 'raising' => 'raise',
    'lowering' => 'lower', 'squeezing' => 'squeeze', 'shaking' => 'shake',
    'kissing' => 'kiss', 'biting' => 'bite', 'wearing' => 'wear', 'clenching' => 'clench'
  }.freeze

  # Body words we accept from INSPECT and from `worn:` queries.  Longest first so that
  # "shoulders" wins over "shoulder" and "ears" over "ear" -- an earring the game hangs from
  # one ear and a pair that takes both are different chart rows with different limits, and the
  # plural in the INSPECT phrase ("hanging it from your ear" vs "hanging them from your ears")
  # is the only thing that says which.  "pin" last so a real body word always wins over it.
  SLOTS = %w[shoulders shoulder head hair ears ear eyes face neck throat back chest front
             torso arms arm wrists wrist hands hand fingers finger waist belt legs leg
             ankles ankle feet foot tail pin].freeze

  # Words a query may use for a worn location that aren't body words.  `2ear` is how you ask
  # for the both-ears slot: "ears" reads like the plural of "ear" rather than a different place
  # to wear something, so it's a poor thing to have to type the difference with.
  SLOT_QUERY_WORDS = (SLOTS + %w[2ear 2ears bothears]).freeze

  # What INSPECT says about a pin-worn item: "You determine that you could wear the badge
  # anywhere on your body."  That's the pin slot, not a body part.
  PIN_WORN = /anywhere on your body/

  # Canonical slot for the plural/variant forms above.  "ears" is deliberately not folded into
  # "ear": those are chart rows 16 and 17, each with its own limit, so a pair that takes both
  # ears competes with other pairs rather than with single studs.
  SLOT_ALIASES = { 'ears' => '2ear', '2ears' => '2ear', 'bothears' => '2ear',
                   'wrists' => 'wrist', 'fingers' => 'finger',
                   'shoulder' => 'shoulders', 'arm' => 'arms', 'hand' => 'hands',
                   'leg' => 'legs', 'ankles' => 'ankle', 'foot' => 'feet',
                   'belt' => 'waist', 'throat' => 'neck' }.freeze

  # Fallback when INSPECT doesn't tell us where a thing is worn.  Marked as inferred.  A plural
  # "earrings" is a pair, which is the both-ears slot; a single "earring" hangs from one ear.
  NOUN_SLOTS = {
    'earring' => 'ear', 'earrings' => '2ear', 'earcuff' => 'ear', 'earstud' => 'ear',
    'necklace' => 'neck', 'neckchain' => 'neck', 'torc' => 'neck', 'torque' => 'neck',
    'pendant' => 'neck', 'medallion' => 'neck', 'amulet' => 'neck', 'talisman' => 'neck',
    'choker' => 'neck', 'collar' => 'neck', 'locket' => 'neck', 'chain' => 'neck',
    'bracelet' => 'wrist', 'bracer' => 'wrist', 'bracers' => 'wrist', 'armband' => 'wrist',
    'wristband' => 'wrist', 'bangle' => 'wrist', 'cuff' => 'wrist',
    'ring' => 'finger', 'band' => 'finger', 'signet' => 'finger',
    'crown' => 'head', 'circlet' => 'head', 'tiara' => 'head', 'diadem' => 'head',
    'coronet' => 'head', 'headband' => 'head', 'cap' => 'head', 'hat' => 'head',
    'helm' => 'head', 'helmet' => 'head', 'hood' => 'head', 'veil' => 'head',
    'barrette' => 'hair', 'comb' => 'hair', 'hairpin' => 'hair', 'ribbon' => 'hair',
    'pin' => 'pin', 'stickpin' => 'pin', 'brooch' => 'pin', 'badge' => 'pin',
    'emblem' => 'pin', 'clasp' => 'pin',
    'belt' => 'waist', 'buckle' => 'waist', 'sash' => 'waist', 'girdle' => 'waist',
    'anklet' => 'ankle', 'gloves' => 'hands', 'gauntlets' => 'hands',
    'boots' => 'feet', 'shoes' => 'feet', 'sandals' => 'feet', 'slippers' => 'feet',
    'cloak' => 'shoulders', 'cape' => 'shoulders', 'mantle' => 'shoulders',
    'shirt' => 'torso', 'robe' => 'torso', 'robes' => 'torso', 'tunic' => 'torso',
    'doublet' => 'torso', 'vest' => 'torso', 'greaves' => 'legs', 'aventail' => 'neck'
  }.freeze

  # Checked before NOUN_SLOTS -- "arm greaves" and "leg greaves" share a noun.
  NAME_SLOT_HINTS = [
    [/\barm (?:greaves|guards|bands?)\b/, 'arms'],
    [/\bleg (?:greaves|guards)\b/,        'legs'],
    [/\bcloak (?:clasp|pin)\b/,           'shoulders']
  ].freeze

  # The game's worn slots, as INVENTORY USAGE lists them.  Keyed by that chart's CODE column,
  # which is the stable part of it -- a row can be reworded, its number can't.
  #
  # There are deliberately no limits here: how many functional items a row holds depends on the
  # account's subscription, so the only honest source for them is the game itself.  Usage reads
  # the FUNC column of INVENTORY USAGE for that (the TOTAL column also counts items that do
  # nothing, and every enhancive is functional, so the second column is no use here), and a
  # slot has no limit to show until that has been read.
  #
  # `slot` is the canonical worn_location a row feeds, and more than one row can feed the same
  # one -- something around your waist and something attached to your belt are separate rows
  # with separate limits, and INSPECT doesn't always say which of the three leg rows an item
  # would use.  Summaries by body area therefore add the rows up (and say when they did);
  # `;itemdb usage` shows the chart row by row, exactly as the game reports it.  The two ear
  # rows are the other way round -- one body part, two slots -- because the game counts an
  # earring hung from one ear separately from a pair that takes both.
  WORN_SLOTS = [
    { code: 1,  slot: 'pin',       desc: 'as a pin' },
    { code: 2,  slot: 'back',      desc: 'on your back' },
    { code: 3,  slot: 'waist',     desc: 'around your waist' },
    { code: 4,  slot: 'head',      desc: 'on your head' },
    { code: 5,  slot: 'shoulders', desc: 'slung over your shoulder' },
    { code: 6,  slot: 'shoulders', desc: 'draped over your shoulders' },
    { code: 7,  slot: 'legs',      desc: 'pulled over your legs' },
    { code: 8,  slot: 'chest',     desc: 'over your chest' },
    { code: 9,  slot: 'wrist',     desc: 'attached to your wrist' },
    { code: 10, slot: 'finger',    desc: 'on your fingers' },
    { code: 11, slot: 'feet',      desc: 'on your feet' },
    { code: 12, slot: 'neck',      desc: 'hung around your neck' },
    { code: 13, slot: 'waist',     desc: 'attached to your belt' },
    { code: 14, slot: 'arms',      desc: 'attached to your arms' },
    { code: 15, slot: 'legs',      desc: 'attached to your legs' },
    { code: 16, slot: 'ear',       desc: 'hung from a single ear' },
    { code: 17, slot: '2ear',      desc: 'hung from both ears' },
    { code: 18, slot: 'ankle',     desc: 'attached to your ankle' },
    { code: 19, slot: 'front',     desc: 'put over your front' },
    { code: 20, slot: 'hands',     desc: 'slipped over your hands' },
    { code: 21, slot: 'feet',      desc: 'slipped on your feet' },
    { code: 22, slot: 'hair',      desc: 'placed in your hair' },
    { code: 23, slot: 'chest',     desc: 'slipped into, on your chest' },
    { code: 24, slot: 'legs',      desc: 'slipped into, on your legs' }
  ].freeze

  # Weighting / padding / sighting (WPS).  A loresong reports the treatment with a degree word
  # ("very heavily padded", "somewhat sighted"), and each word covers a band of the Combat
  # Effectiveness Rating (CER) -- the loresong never reveals the exact CER inside its band.  So
  # "very heavily" means CER 11-13; the item might be any of those.  Ordered strongest-last;
  # negative bands are diminished (worse than baseline) and have no service cost to apply.
  # [degree word, cer_min, cer_max].
  WPS_TIERS = [
    ['substantially diminished', -50, -16],
    ['noticeably diminished',    -15, -11],
    ['somewhat diminished',      -10,  -6],
    ['slightly diminished',       -5,  -1],
    ['lightly',                    1,   2],
    ['fairly',                     3,   4],
    ['somewhat',                   5,   6],
    ['decently',                   7,   8],
    ['heavily',                    9,  10],
    ['very heavily',              11,  13],
    ['exceptionally',             14,  15],
    ['masterfully',               16,  20],
    ['superbly',                  21,  25],
    ['expertly',                  26,  30],
    ['phenomenally',              31,  35],
    ['fantastically',             36,  40],
    ['incredibly',                41,  45],
    ['wondrously',                46,  50]
  ].freeze

  # CER => the number of services (bloodscrip, off a Warrior Guild NPC, etc.) it costs to bring a
  # treatment up to that rating.  Not a formula -- the game's own price chart, so it's tabulated.
  WPS_SERVICES = {
    1 => 10,   2 => 20,   3 => 30,   4 => 40,   5 => 50,
    6 => 70,   7 => 90,   8 => 110,  9 => 130,  10 => 150,
    11 => 180, 12 => 210, 13 => 240, 14 => 270, 15 => 300,
    16 => 340, 17 => 380, 18 => 420, 19 => 460, 20 => 500,
    21 => 600, 22 => 700, 23 => 800, 24 => 900, 25 => 1000,
    26 => 1100, 27 => 1200, 28 => 1300, 29 => 1400, 30 => 1500,
    31 => 1600, 32 => 1700, 33 => 1800, 34 => 1900, 35 => 2000,
    36 => 2100, 37 => 2200, 38 => 2300, 39 => 2400, 40 => 2500,
    41 => 2700, 42 => 2900, 43 => 3100, 44 => 3300, 45 => 3500,
    46 => 3800, 47 => 4100, 48 => 4400, 49 => 4700, 50 => 5000
  }.freeze

  # The degree word off a loresong => its [cer_min, cer_max] band, or nil if we don't know it.
  def self.wps_band(degree)
    row = WPS_TIERS.find { |name, _lo, _hi| name == degree.to_s.strip.downcase }
    row && [row[1], row[2]]
  end

  # "very heavily" => "very heavily (CER 11-13, ~180-240 services)".  Unknown words pass through
  # raw so a phrasing we haven't tabulated is still shown rather than dropped.  Diminished bands
  # (negative CER) show the rating but no service cost, since you don't pay to make gear worse.
  def self.wps_summary(degree)
    lo, hi = wps_band(degree)
    return degree.to_s if lo.nil?
    cer = lo == hi ? lo.to_s : "#{lo} to #{hi}"
    svc = [WPS_SERVICES[lo], WPS_SERVICES[hi]].compact
    if svc.length == 2
      "#{degree} (CER #{cer}, ~#{svc.first}-#{svc.last} services)"
    else
      "#{degree} (CER #{cer})"
    end
  end

  # Query abbreviations.  Keys are squashed (letters/digits only) so "el fire", "elfire"
  # and "el-fire" all land on the same entry.
  ALIASES = {
    'str' => 'Strength', 'con' => 'Constitution', 'dex' => 'Dexterity',
    'agi' => 'Agility', 'dis' => 'Discipline', 'disc' => 'Discipline',
    'aur' => 'Aura', 'log' => 'Logic', 'int' => 'Intuition', 'wis' => 'Wisdom',
    'inf' => 'Influence',
    'twc' => 'Two Weapon Combat', 'cm' => 'Combat Maneuvers', 'cman' => 'Combat Maneuvers',
    'thw' => 'Two-Handed Weapons', 'twohanded' => 'Two-Handed Weapons',
    'moc' => 'Multi Opponent Combat', 'pf' => 'Physical Fitness', 'fitness' => 'Physical Fitness',
    'dodge' => 'Dodging', 'armor' => 'Armor Use', 'shield' => 'Shield Use',
    'edged' => 'Edged Weapons', 'blunt' => 'Blunt Weapons', 'ranged' => 'Ranged Weapons',
    'thrown' => 'Thrown Weapons', 'polearm' => 'Polearm Weapons', 'brawl' => 'Brawling',
    'as' => 'Arcane Symbols', 'arcane' => 'Arcane Symbols', 'miu' => 'Magic Item Use',
    'sa' => 'Spell Aiming', 'aiming' => 'Spell Aiming', 'harness' => 'Harness Power',
    'emc' => 'Elemental Mana Control', 'mmc' => 'Mental Mana Control',
    'smc' => 'Spirit Mana Control',
    'elair' => 'Elemental Lore - Air', 'elearth' => 'Elemental Lore - Earth',
    'elfire' => 'Elemental Lore - Fire', 'elwater' => 'Elemental Lore - Water',
    'slb' => 'Spiritual Lore - Blessings', 'slr' => 'Spiritual Lore - Religion',
    'sls' => 'Spiritual Lore - Summoning', 'sld' => 'Sorcerous Lore - Demonology',
    'sln' => 'Sorcerous Lore - Necromancy',
    'mld' => 'Mental Lore - Divination', 'mlm' => 'Mental Lore - Manipulation',
    'mlt' => 'Mental Lore - Telepathy', 'mltr' => 'Mental Lore - Transference',
    'mltf' => 'Mental Lore - Transformation',
    'dt' => 'Disarming Traps', 'disarm' => 'Disarming Traps',
    'pl' => 'Picking Locks', 'picking' => 'Picking Locks', 'lockpicking' => 'Picking Locks',
    'sh' => 'Stalking and Hiding', 'stalking' => 'Stalking and Hiding',
    'stalk' => 'Stalking and Hiding', 'hiding' => 'Stalking and Hiding',
    'hide' => 'Stalking and Hiding', 'stalkingandhiding' => 'Stalking and Hiding',
    'perc' => 'Perception', 'fa' => 'First Aid', 'aid' => 'First Aid',
    'pp' => 'Pickpocketing', 'pickpocket' => 'Pickpocketing', 'steal' => 'Pickpocketing',
    'climb' => 'Climbing', 'swim' => 'Swimming', 'trade' => 'Trading',
    'hpr' => 'Health Recovery', 'mpr' => 'Mana Recovery', 'spr' => 'Stamina Recovery',
    'maxhp' => 'Max Health', 'maxmana' => 'Max Mana', 'maxstam' => 'Max Stamina'
  }.freeze

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------- text helpers

  # Letters and digits only -- used for fuzzy name matching so that punctuation and
  # spacing in "Spiritual Lore - Summoning" never gets in the way.
  def self.squash(str)
    str.to_s.downcase.gsub(/[^a-z0-9]/, '')
  end

  # A resistance's damage type as the loresong words it => the canonical type.  "natural attacks"
  # is the nature type; everything else drops a trailing "attacks"/"attack" and is used as named
  # (acid, cold, crush, disintegration, electrical, fire, impact, plasma, vacuum, ...).  Shared by
  # the parser (storing) and Query (matching resist:<type>), so it lives up here with squash.
  def self.normalize_resist(phrase)
    type = phrase.to_s.downcase.sub(/\s+attacks?\z/, '').strip
    type == 'natural' ? 'nature' : type
  end

  def self.commify(num)
    num.to_i.to_s.reverse.scan(/\d{1,3}/).join(',').reverse
  end

  # 400000 -> "400k", 2050000 -> "2.05m"
  def self.short_silver(num)
    return '' if num.nil?
    if num >= 1_000_000
      format('%gm', (num / 1_000_000.0).round(2))
    elsif num >= 1_000
      format('%gk', (num / 1_000.0).round(1))
    else
      num.to_s
    end
  end

  # "500k" / "1.5m" / "500,000" -> integer
  def self.parse_silver(str)
    str = str.to_s.delete(',').strip
    case str
    when /\A(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)k\z/i then ($1.to_f * 1_000).round
    when /\A(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)m\z/i then ($1.to_f * 1_000_000).round
    when /\A\d+(?:\.\d+)?\z/     then str.to_f.round
    end
  end

  def self.canonical_slot(word)
    word = word.to_s.downcase
    SLOT_ALIASES[word] || word
  end

  # Game item ids are only good for the current login, so entries are keyed by a slug of the
  # item's name instead:  "some ornate mithril leg greaves" -> "ornate-mithril-leg-greaves".
  def self.slug(name)
    base = name.to_s.downcase
               .sub(/\A(?:an?|some|the)\s+/, '')
               .sub(/\Apair of\s+/, '')
               .gsub(/[^a-z0-9]+/, '-')
               .gsub(/\A-+|-+\z/, '')
    base.empty? ? 'item' : base
  end

  # Flares are stored as { type, temporary, message }.  Bare strings are tolerated so a db
  # written by an older version still reads.
  def self.flares(item)
    Array(item['flares']).map { |f| f.is_a?(Hash) ? f : { 'type' => f.to_s, 'message' => f.to_s } }
  end

  def self.flare_labels(item)
    flares(item).map { |f| f['temporary'] ? "#{f['type']} (temporary)" : f['type'] }
  end

  # ============================================================================== Parser
  #
  # Turns the plain text of RECALL (plus optionally INSPECT) into a hash.  Pure string
  # work -- no game interaction -- so it can be exercised against a log file.

  module Parser
    def self.parse(recall_lines, inspect_lines = [], analyze_lines = [])
      item = {
        'status'     => 'ok',
        'recall_name' => nil,
        'loresong_by' => nil,
        'worth'      => nil,
        'weight_lbs' => nil,
        'weight_desc' => nil,
        'enchant'    => nil,
        'flares'     => [],
        'resistances' => {},
        'material'   => nil,
        'flags'      => {},
        'magic'      => nil,
        'enhancive'  => nil
      }
      unrecognized = []
      boosts       = nil   # non-nil once we're inside the enhancive block

      recall_lines.each do |raw|
        line = raw.to_s.rstrip
        next if line.empty?
        indented = line.start_with?(' ')
        text = line.strip

        case text

        # ---- the opening line, which is also where the short name comes from
        when /\AAs you recall (?:(.+?)'s|the (.+?)'s|a (\w+)'s) song, you feel .*? from (?:the|your) (.+?) in your hand/
          item['loresong_by'] = ($1 || $2 || $3)
          item['recall_name'] = $4
        when /\AAs you recall .*? song, you feel .*? from (?:the|your) (.+?) in your hand/
          item['recall_name'] = $1
        when /\AYou are unable to recall the (.+?)'s loresong/
          item['recall_name'] = $1
          item['status']      = 'no-loresong'
        when /\AYou (?:sense|feel) nothing (?:unusual|special)/
          item['status'] = 'no-loresong'

        # ---- magical / imbedded spell
        when /\AIt is currently imbedded with the (.+?) spell\./
          magic(item)['spell'] = $1
        when /\AIt is a magical item which could be imbedded with a spell\./
          magic(item)['spell'] = nil
        when /\AIt has (\d+) charges?\./
          magic(item)['charges']       = $1.to_i
          magic(item)['rechargeable']  = true if text.include?('It is rechargeable')
          if text =~ /empowered and can be charged for an additional (\d+)/
            magic(item)['empowered']    = true
            magic(item)['max_recharge'] = $1.to_i
          end
        when /\AIt could be activated by (.+?)\.\z/
          full   = Regexp.last_match(1)
          gerund = full.sub(/ it\z/, '')
          magic(item)['activator']        = ItemDB::ACTIVATORS[gerund] || gerund
          magic(item)['activator_phrase'] = full
        when /\AIt will (persist|disintegrate|crumble into dust) after its last magical charge has been expended\./
          magic(item)['after_last_charge'] = (Regexp.last_match(1) == 'persist' ? 'persist' : 'disintegrate')

        # ---- enhancive block
        when /\AIt is an enhancive item:/
          boosts = []
          item['enhancive'] = { 'boosts' => boosts }
        when /\AIt provides a boost of (\d+) to (.+?)\.\z/
          boost = build_boost($1.to_i, $2)
          if boosts.nil?   # defensive: boost line without the header
            boosts = []
            item['enhancive'] = { 'boosts' => boosts }
          end
          boosts << boost
        when /\AThis enhancement may not be used by adventurers who have not trained (\d+) times?\./
          boosts&.last&.[]=('min_trained', $1.to_i)
        when /\AIt seems to have (.+?) enhancive charges? remaining\./
          apply_charges(item, $1)
        when /\AIt will (persist|crumble into dust) after its last enhancive charge has been expended\./
          item['enhancive'] ||= { 'boosts' => (boosts ||= []) }
          item['enhancive']['after_last_charge'] = ($1 == 'persist' ? 'persist' : 'crumble')

        # ---- enchant and flares
        when /\AIt imparts a bonus of \+(\d+) more than usual\./
          item['enchant'] = $1.to_i
        when /\AIt may be enchanted up to a bonus of (\d+)/
          item['max_enchant']  = Regexp.last_match(1).to_i
          item['enchanted_by'] = Regexp.last_match(1) if text =~ /last enchanted by (.+?)\.\z/
        when /\AIt (?:has|is) .*?infused with/, /\AThe harmonics generated tell you/,
             /\AProtects against magical attacks\.\z/
          flare = classify_flare(text)
          flare ? item['flares'] << flare : unrecognized << text
        when /\AIt (?:radiates|pulses with|is imbued with) .*?\bflare/
          item['flares'] << { 'type' => 'unknown', 'message' => text }

        # ---- weight / worth / materials / misc flags
        when /\AIt is a (small|tiny) item, under a pound\./
          item['weight_desc'] = text.sub(/\AIt is /, '').chomp('.')
        when /\AIt appears to weigh about (\d+) pounds?\./
          item['weight_lbs']  = $1.to_i
          item['weight_desc'] = "about #{$1} pounds"
        when /\AIt is estimated to be worth about ([\d,]+) silvers?\./
          item['worth'] = $1.delete(',').to_i
        when /\AIt (?:has no|is of no) (?:apparent )?(?:value|worth)/
          item['worth'] = 0
        when /\AIt is predominantly crafted of (.+?)\.\z/
          item['material'] = $1
        when /\AIt is an? (\w+) project \((\d+) difficulty\) for an adventurer to modify\./
          item['flags']['alter_class']      = $1
          item['flags']['alter_difficulty'] = $2.to_i
        when /\AIt is magic resistant\./          then item['flags']['magic_resistant'] = true
        when /\AIt is a holy item\./              then item['flags']['holy'] = true
        when /\AIt is an unholy item\./           then item['flags']['unholy'] = true
        # Damage resistance, e.g. "It is resistant (95%) to natural attacks." -- "natural" is the
        # nature damage type.  Kept as { type => percent } so it's searchable with resist:<type>.
        when /\AIt (?:is|appears to be) resistant \((\d+)%\) to (.+?)\.\z/
          item['resistances'][ItemDB.normalize_resist(Regexp.last_match(2))] = Regexp.last_match(1).to_i
        # Ensorcell / sanctify tiers.  A trailing sentence can ride along on the same line.
        when /\AIt has been ensorcelled (\d+) times?\.(.*)\z/
          item['flags']['ensorcelled'] = Regexp.last_match(1).to_i
          (rest = Regexp.last_match(2).to_s.strip).empty? || unrecognized << rest
        when /\AIt has been sanctified (\d+) times?\.(.*)\z/
          item['flags']['sanctified'] = Regexp.last_match(1).to_i
          rest = Regexp.last_match(2).to_s.strip
          if rest =~ /Holy Fire flares/ then add_holy_fire(item)
          elsif !rest.empty?            then unrecognized << rest
          end
        # The top (6th) tier of sanctification shows as a permanent Holy Fire flare, so a line of
        # its own -- or the tail of the sanctify line above -- both mean tier 6.
        when /\AIt has .*Holy Fire flares/
          add_holy_fire(item)
        # Weapon/armor treatments come graded ("very heavily padded", "somewhat sighted"), so
        # capture the degree word between "It is" and the keyword; it names the CER band (see
        # WPS_TIERS).  "temporarily" marks a spell-granted one and isn't part of the degree.
        when /\AIt is (.*?)padded against critical blows/
          record_treatment(item, 'padded', 'padding_temporary', $1, text)
        when /\AIt is (.*?)weighted for/
          record_treatment(item, 'weighted', 'weighting_temporary', $1, text)
        when /\AIt is (.*?)sighted to assist in aiming/
          record_treatment(item, 'sighted', 'sighting_temporary', $1, text)
        when /\AIt has a permanently unlocked loresong by (?:a |an |the )?(.+?)\.\z/
          item['loresong_by'] ||= $1
        when /\AIt (?:can|cannot|could not) be/, /\AIt is not/
          (item['flags']['notes'] ||= []) << text

        else
          unrecognized << (indented ? "    #{text}" : text)
        end
      end

      parse_inspect(item, inspect_lines)
      parse_analyze(item, analyze_lines)

      item['flags'].delete_if { |_k, v| v.nil? }
      item.delete('flags') if item['flags'].empty?
      item.delete('flares') if item['flares'].empty?
      item.delete('resistances') if item['resistances'].empty?
      item['unrecognized'] = unrecognized unless unrecognized.empty?
      item
    end

    # INSPECT is the only thing that reports where an item is worn.
    def self.parse_inspect(item, lines)
      text = Array(lines).join(' ')
      return if text.empty?

      if text =~ /You determine that you could (?:wear|pin|attach|clip|tie|fasten) (.+?)\./
        phrase = Regexp.last_match(1)
        slot   = if phrase =~ ItemDB::PIN_WORN
                   'pin'
                 else
                   ItemDB::SLOTS.find { |w| phrase =~ /\b#{w}\b/ }
                 end
        if slot
          item['worn_location'] = ItemDB.canonical_slot(slot)
          item['worn_phrase']   = phrase
          item['worn_source']   = 'inspect'
        end
      end
      item['flags'] = (item['flags'] || {}).merge('serves_a_purpose' => true) if text =~ /appears? to serve some purpose/
    end

    # ANALYZE, not the loresong, is where a spell-specialization book keeps its detail: how many
    # specialization ranks READing it grants, and to which spell (name and number).  The book is
    # consumed when READ.  A book's recall usually reads as "no loresong", so finding the spec
    # here is what makes the entry worth having -- its status is set back to ok.
    def self.parse_analyze(item, lines)
      text = Array(lines).join(' ')
      return if text.empty?

      if text =~ /add\s+(\d+)\s+spell\s+specialization\s+ranks?\s+to\s+casting\s+the\s+spell\s+(.+?)\s*\((\d+)\)/i
        item['spell_spec'] = {
          'spell'  => Regexp.last_match(2).strip,
          'number' => Regexp.last_match(3).to_i,
          'ranks'  => Regexp.last_match(1).to_i
        }
        item['status'] = 'ok'
      end
    end

    # Fall back to the item's noun when INSPECT gave us nothing.
    def self.infer_worn_location(item, full_name, noun)
      return if item['worn_location']
      name = full_name.to_s.downcase

      hint = ItemDB::NAME_SLOT_HINTS.find { |re, _slot| name =~ re }
      slot = hint ? hint[1] : ItemDB::NOUN_SLOTS[noun.to_s.downcase]
      slot ||= ItemDB::NOUN_SLOTS[name.split.last.to_s]
      return unless slot

      item['worn_location'] = slot
      item['worn_source']   = 'noun'
    end

    def self.magic(item)
      item['magic'] ||= {}
    end

    # Store a weapon/armor treatment (padding / weighting / sighting).  `phrase` is whatever sat
    # between "It is" and the keyword -- e.g. "very heavily " or "temporarily somewhat ".  We keep
    # the degree word (which names the CER band, per WPS_TIERS) as the flag's value; "temporarily"
    # is pulled out into its own flag.  A treatment with no degree word (shouldn't happen, but be
    # safe) stores `true` so the item still reads as treated.
    def self.record_treatment(item, key, temp_key, phrase, text)
      item['flags'][temp_key] = true if text.include?('temporarily')
      degree = phrase.to_s.sub(/\btemporarily\b/, '').strip.downcase
      item['flags'][key] = degree.empty? ? true : degree
    end

    # A permanent Holy Fire flare is how the 6th (top) tier of sanctification reads -- the line
    # never says "sanctified 6 times" -- so record both the tier and the flare itself.
    def self.add_holy_fire(item)
      item['flags']['sanctified'] = 6
      return if item['flares'].any? { |f| f.is_a?(Hash) && f['type'] == 'holy fire' }
      item['flares'] << { 'type' => 'holy fire', 'message' => 'It has permanent Holy Fire flares.' }
    end

    # "Spiritual Lore - Blessings Bonus" -> skill/bonus, "Strength Base" -> stat/base,
    # "Max Mana" -> resource.  Only stats have a Base, only stats and skills have a Bonus.
    def self.build_boost(value, target)
      if target =~ /\A(.*?)\s+(Base|Bonus|Ranks)\z/
        name = Regexp.last_match(1)
        type = Regexp.last_match(2).downcase
      else
        name = target
        type = nil
      end

      category = if ItemDB::STAT_NAMES.include?(name) then 'stat'
                 elsif type.nil?                     then 'resource'
                 else                                     'skill'
                 end

      boost = { 'name' => name, 'category' => category, 'type' => type, 'value' => value }
      # A stat's cap is expressed in base points and 1 bonus point is worth 2 of them.
      boost['base_equiv'] = (type == 'bonus' ? value * 2 : value) if category == 'stat'
      boost['cap'] = case category
                     when 'stat'  then ItemDB::STAT_CAP
                     when 'skill' then ItemDB::SKILL_CAP
                     end
      boost.compact
    end

    # "You are unable to recall the green orb's loresong.  Perhaps a bard should work on it
    # first?" -- nobody has unlocked this item, so there is nothing to read off it.
    # (Regexp.union, not one /x pattern -- /x would eat the literal spaces.)
    NO_LORESONG = Regexp.union(
      /unable to recall the .*?loresong/,
      /Perhaps a bard should work on it first/,
      /\AYou (?:sense|feel) nothing (?:unusual|special)/
    ).freeze

    def self.locked?(lines)
      Array(lines).any? { |line| line.to_s =~ NO_LORESONG }
    end

    # Ways the game declines a recall outright: the item is out of reach (in a locker you
    # aren't holding it from), or it couldn't work out what you meant.  These have to be
    # captured -- otherwise the scan waits out the timeout on every item -- but they are not
    # data, and an entry parsed from one would claim a successful, entirely featureless
    # loresong.  "can't get close enough" is what a locker item gives when it isn't in hand.
    REFUSED = Regexp.union(
      /You can't get close enough/,
      /What were you referring to\?/,
      /I could not find what you were referring to/,
      /Recall what\?/,
      /Inspect what\?/,
      /You have no idea how to (?:recall|inspect)/
    ).freeze

    def self.refused?(lines)
      Array(lines).any? { |line| line.to_s =~ REFUSED }
    end

    # Turns a flare message into { type, temporary, message }.  nil if the wording isn't one
    # we know, so the caller can file the line under unrecognized instead of guessing.
    def self.classify_flare(text)
      match = ItemDB::FLARE_MESSAGES.find { |pattern, _name| text =~ pattern }
      return nil unless match

      flare = { 'type' => match[1], 'message' => text }
      flare['temporary'] = true if text =~ /temporaril/i
      flare
    end

    def self.apply_charges(item, phrase)
      phrase = phrase.strip
      item['enhancive'] ||= { 'boosts' => [] }
      item['enhancive']['charges_phrase'] = phrase

      # A good enough loresong reports the real count rather than a phrase.
      if phrase =~ /\A(\d+)\z/
        exact = Regexp.last_match(1).to_i
        item['enhancive']['charges']      = exact
        item['enhancive']['charges_min']  = exact
        item['enhancive']['charges_max']  = exact
        item['enhancive']['charges_tier'] = ItemDB::CHARGE_TIERS.values
                                                  .find { |(_t, lo, hi)| exact >= lo && (hi.nil? || exact <= hi) }
                                                  &.first
        return
      end

      key = ItemDB::CHARGE_TIERS.keys.find { |k| phrase == k || phrase.start_with?(k) || k.start_with?(phrase) }
      tier, min, max = ItemDB::CHARGE_TIERS[key] if key
      item['enhancive']['charges_tier'] = tier
      item['enhancive']['charges_min']  = min
      item['enhancive']['charges_max']  = max
      item['enhancive']['charges']      = min if min && min == max
    end
  end

  # =============================================================================== Store

  module Store
    def self.file
      @file ||= File.join(DATA_DIR, 'itemdb.yaml')
    end

    def self.data
      @data ||= load
    end

    def self.load
      return blank unless File.exist?(file)
      loaded = YAML.load_file(file)
      return blank unless loaded.is_a?(Hash) && loaded['items'].is_a?(Hash)
      migrate(loaded)
    rescue StandardError => e
      ItemDB.warn "could not read #{file}: #{e.message}"
      blank
    end

    # Databases written by version 1.0.0 are keyed by the item id the game happened to hand
    # out that login, which identifies nothing today.  Re-key them by name so those scans
    # aren't wasted; the old id is kept as item_id, same as a fresh scan would record it.
    def self.migrated?
      @migrated
    end

    def self.migrate(loaded)
      resplit_ears(rekey(loaded))
    end

    def self.rekey(loaded)
      return loaded if loaded['items'].each_value.all? { |e| e.is_a?(Hash) && e['key'] }

      rekeyed = {}
      loaded['items'].each do |old_key, entry|
        next unless entry.is_a?(Hash)

        entry = entry.dup
        entry['item_id'] ||= entry.delete('id') || old_key
        base = ItemDB.slug(entry['name'])
        key = base
        n = 1
        key = "#{base}-#{n += 1}" while rekeyed.key?(key)
        entry['key'] = key
        rekeyed[key] = ItemDB.ordered_entry(entry)
      end
      @migrated = true
      ItemDB.info "re-keyed #{rekeyed.size} entries by item name (item ids don't survive a relog)"
      loaded.merge('items' => rekeyed)
    end

    # Up to 1.3.0 both earring rows were recorded as 'ear'.  They have separate limits, so they
    # are separate slots now: an entry whose INSPECT phrase says both ears moves across, and one
    # that was only ever guessed from a noun goes by whether that noun was a pair.  Anything
    # INSPECT called a single ear stays where it is.
    def self.resplit_ears(loaded)
      moved = 0
      loaded['items'].each_value do |entry|
        next unless entry.is_a?(Hash) && entry['worn_location'] == 'ear'

        both = if entry['worn_phrase']
                 entry['worn_phrase'] =~ /\bears\b/
               else
                 ItemDB::NOUN_SLOTS[entry['noun'].to_s.downcase] == '2ear'
               end
        next unless both

        entry['worn_location'] = '2ear'
        moved += 1
      end
      return loaded if moved.zero?

      @migrated = true
      ItemDB.info "moved #{moved} entr#{moved == 1 ? 'y' : 'ies'} from ear to 2ear " \
                  '(a pair that takes both ears has its own slot and its own limit)'
      loaded
    end

    # slot_usage is keyed by character name -- see Usage.
    def self.blank
      { 'version' => ItemDB::VERSION, 'last_container' => nil, 'slot_usage' => {}, 'items' => {} }
    end

    def self.items
      data['items']
    end

    def self.save
      data['version'] = ItemDB::VERSION
      tmp = "#{file}.tmp"
      File.open(tmp, 'w') { |f| f.write(Psych.dump(data, line_width: -1)) }
      File.delete(file) if File.exist?(file)
      File.rename(tmp, file)
      true
    rescue StandardError => e
      ItemDB.warn "could not write #{file}: #{e.message}"
      false
    end

    def self.backup
      return nil unless File.exist?(file)
      dest = "#{file}.bak"
      File.write(dest, File.read(file))
      dest
    end

    # Entries nothing could be read off.  A normal scan leaves them alone; `scan ... retry`
    # picks them back up, for after a bard has unlocked them.
    RETRY_STATUS = %w[no-loresong unreadable].freeze

    def self.retryable?(key)
      RETRY_STATUS.include?(items[key.to_s].to_h['status'])
    end

    # Entry keys grouped by name slug, in the order they were recorded.  Narrowed to one
    # container when given, since that's the set a scan of it compares against.
    def self.keys_by_slug(container_name = nil)
      grouped = {}
      items.each do |key, entry|
        next if container_name && entry['container'] != container_name

        (grouped[ItemDB.slug(entry['name'])] ||= []) << key
      end
      grouped
    end

    # "a gold ring" -> "gold-ring", then "gold-ring-2", "gold-ring-3" for further copies.
    def self.next_key(name)
      base = ItemDB.slug(name)
      return base unless items.key?(base)

      n = 2
      n += 1 while items.key?("#{base}-#{n}")
      "#{base}-#{n}"
    end

    def self.put(key, entry)
      items[key.to_s] = entry
    end

    def self.delete(key)
      items.delete(key.to_s)
    end

    # Every distinct boost name we've ever recorded -- the universe fuzzy search matches
    # against, so a query can never resolve to something that isn't in the db.
    def self.boost_names
      items.values.flat_map { |i| Array(i.dig('enhancive', 'boosts')).map { |b| b['name'] } }.compact.uniq
    end
  end

  # ============================================================================= Scanner

  module Scanner
    # The game doesn't always lead with "As you recall ...'s song" -- sometimes the output
    # starts straight in on the item's properties -- so the start pattern has to accept any
    # plausible first line of a loresong recall.
    # Built with Regexp.union rather than one /x-extended pattern: /x ignores unescaped
    # whitespace, which would quietly turn "As you recall" into "Asyourecall" and make the
    # capture match nothing at all.
    RECALL_START = Regexp.union(
      /As you recall .*?song/,
      /You are unable to recall the .*?loresong/,
      /Perhaps a bard should work on it first/,
      /You have no idea how to recall/,
      /Recall what\?/,
      /You can't get close enough/,
      /What were you referring to\?/,
      /I could not find what you were referring to/,
      /\AYou (?:sense|feel) nothing (?:unusual|special)/,
      /\bIt (?:is|has|imparts|appears|could|will|provides)\s/
    ).freeze

    # The refusals matter as much as the successes: if nothing here matches, the capture sits
    # there until it times out, so every way the game can decline an inspect needs a pattern.
    INSPECT_START = Regexp.union(
      /You carefully inspect/,
      /You have no idea how to inspect/,
      /Inspect what\?/,
      /Inspecting that may not be a sound idea/,
      /You can't get close enough/,
      /What were you referring to\?/,
      /You determine that you could/
    ).freeze

    # ANALYZE is the only place a spell-specialization book keeps its detail -- its loresong
    # shows nothing useful -- so a book/codex is analyzed where it sits (no get/put needed, same
    # as recall).  As with the others, every way the game can decline needs a pattern here or the
    # capture just times out.
    ANALYZE_START = Regexp.union(
      /You analyze/,
      /You have no idea how to analyze/,
      /Analyze what\?/,
      /You can't get close enough/,
      /What were you referring to\?/,
      /I could not find what you were referring to/
    ).freeze

    # RECALL reads an item where it sits, in a container you're wearing or one standing in the
    # room, but INSPECT needs it in hand -- so every item costs a get/put pair unless the scan
    # is run without inspect, or the recall came back with nothing worth inspecting for.
    @inspect       = true
    @emptied_hands = false

    def self.inspect?
      @inspect
    end

    # Everything we could plausibly be asked to scan.  A locker's containers aren't in
    # GameObj.inv -- they're room objects, listed under "You also see" (loot) or built into
    # the room description (a chest of drawers in a house), so all three registries count.
    # Inventory comes first so a worn container always wins a tie.
    def self.containers_in_reach
      (GameObj.inv || []) + (GameObj.loot || []) + (GameObj.room_desc || [])
    end

    # Finds a container by name, e.g. "backpack", "tan leather backpack", "bin", "deep chest".
    def self.find_container(name)
      want = name.to_s.downcase.strip
      return nil if want.empty?

      pool = containers_in_reach
      pool.find { |o| o.name.to_s.downcase == want } ||
        pool.find { |o| o.name.to_s.downcase.include?(want) } ||
        pool.find { |o| o.noun.to_s.downcase == want }
    end

    def self.on_body?(container)
      (GameObj.inv || []).any? { |o| o.id.to_s == container.id.to_s }
    end

    # A locker closes when you leave the room (and can be closed under a running script),
    # taking its containers with it.  Once that happens every remaining item would fail, so
    # the scan checks between items rather than grinding through the rest of the list.
    def self.reachable?(container)
      containers_in_reach.any? { |o| o.id.to_s == container.id.to_s }
    end

    # GemStone furniture you set things *on* a surface rather than *in*: an armor stand, a
    # weapon rack, a hook, and so on.  For those, `put X in <it>` fails and leaves the item in
    # your hand, so the get/put/look verbs all need the right preposition.  Matched on the
    # container's noun; anything not listed is an ordinary "in" container.
    ON_SURFACES = %w[stand rack hanger hangers hook hooks peg pegs pole shelf table
                     pedestal mannequin manikin].freeze

    def self.preposition(container)
      ON_SURFACES.include?(container.noun.to_s.downcase) ? 'on' : 'in'
    end

    # A book/codex keeps its spell-specialization detail where ANALYZE reads it, not the
    # loresong, so an item that looks like a book is analyzed during the scan.  This wordlist is
    # a representative sample, not an exhaustive one -- extend it as new book nouns turn up.
    # Matched against the noun and every word of the name, so "bronze-clasped codex" and an
    # oddly-nouned "arcane primer of the edge" both qualify.
    BOOK_WORDS = %w[book codex tome grimoire primer manual folio libram compendium treatise
                    almanac lexicon spellbook workbook handbook journal ledger booklet
                    pamphlet volume].freeze

    def self.book_item?(obj)
      words = "#{obj.noun} #{obj.name}".downcase.scan(/[a-z]+/)
      (words & BOOK_WORDS).any?
    end

    # Lich only knows a container's contents once something has looked inside it, so nudge
    # the game if we come up empty.  nil means it really is closed (or not a container).
    def self.contents(container, probe: true)
      list = GameObj.containers[container.id]
      return list if list
      return nil unless probe

      fput "look #{preposition(container)} ##{container.id}"
      15.times do
        break if GameObj.containers[container.id]
        sleep 0.2
      end
      GameObj.containers[container.id]
    end

    # Runs the whole scan.  refresh: true re-checks items already in the db.
    # inspect_items: false skips the get/inspect/put step and takes worn location from the
    # item's noun instead -- much faster, less accurate.
    # retry_locked: true also re-checks entries nothing could be read off last time.
    # only_name narrows the scan to the items in the container whose name contains it.
    def self.run(container_name, refresh: false, only_name: nil,
                 inspect_items: true, retry_locked: false)
      @inspect = inspect_items
      container = find_container(container_name)
      unless container
        ItemDB.warn "no container matching '#{container_name}' -- in reach: " \
                    "#{containers_in_reach.map(&:name).join(', ')}"
        ItemDB.warn 'if it lives in your locker, open the locker first' unless container_name.to_s.empty?
        return
      end
      # A container standing in the room can disappear mid-scan in a way a worn one can't.
      remote = !on_body?(container)

      list = contents(container)
      if list.nil?
        ItemDB.warn "#{container.name} looks closed (Lich has no contents for it).  Open it and try again."
        return
      end

      # only_name narrows what gets recalled, but the staleness check at the end still has to
      # see everything in the container, or the items we filtered out look like they've gone.
      inside = list
      if only_name
        list = list.select { |o| o.name.to_s.downcase.include?(only_name.downcase) }
        if list.empty?
          ItemDB.warn "nothing called '#{only_name}' is in #{container.name} right now"
          return
        end
      end
      one = !!only_name

      # [item, key] pairs; a nil key means "record this as a new entry".
      todo = refresh || one ? reassign(list, container) : new_and_retries(list, container, retry_locked)

      ItemDB.info "#{container.name}: #{list.size} item#{'s' if list.size != 1} inside, " \
                  "#{todo.size} to #{refresh ? 'refresh' : 'check'}" \
                  "#{" (#{list.size - todo.size} already recorded)" unless refresh || one}" \
                  "#{' -- no inspect, worn location from the item noun' unless @inspect}"
      ItemDB.info "#{container.name} is in the room rather than on you -- leave it open " \
                  'until this finishes' if remote

      # Otherwise the ones we're deliberately not touching are invisible.
      unless refresh || one || retry_locked
        waiting = Store.keys_by_slug(container.name).values.flatten.count { |k| Store.retryable?(k) }
        ItemDB.info "#{waiting} of those gave nothing last time -- " \
                    ";itemdb scan #{container.name} retry to try again" if waiting.positive?
      end

      if todo.empty?
        report_missing(container, inside)
        return
      end

      done   = 0
      unsung = []
      failed = []
      begin
        todo.each_with_index do |(obj, key), idx|
          if remote && !reachable?(container)
            ItemDB.warn "#{container.name} is gone (locker closed?) -- stopping with " \
                        "#{todo.size - idx} item#{'s' if todo.size - idx != 1} unchecked"
            break
          end

          key ||= Store.next_key(obj.name)
          entry = examine(obj, container, key)
          # Every item gets a line, including the ones nothing could be read off -- a run of
          # those with nothing echoed looks exactly like the script having hung.
          case entry['status']
          when 'unreadable'
            failed << obj
            ItemDB.progress(idx + 1, todo.size, obj, entry['reason'] || 'no response to recall')
          when 'no-loresong'
            unsung << obj
            ItemDB.progress(idx + 1, todo.size, obj, 'not loresung yet, needs a bard')
          else
            done += 1
            ItemDB.progress(idx + 1, todo.size, obj, ItemDB.boost_summary(entry))
          end
          Store.save
        end
      ensure
        fill_hands if @emptied_hands
        Store.data['last_container'] = container.name
        Store.save
      end

      ItemDB.info "recorded #{done} item#{'s' if done != 1}" \
                  "#{", #{unsung.size} with no loresong yet (needs a bard; ;itemdb scan #{container.name} retry to check again later)" unless unsung.empty?}" \
                  "#{", #{failed.size} couldn't be read (stored as unreadable; ;itemdb scan #{container.name} retry to try again)" unless failed.empty?}"
      ItemDB.mono "needs a bard: #{unsung.map(&:name).join(', ')}" unless unsung.empty?
      report_missing(container, inside)
    end

    # Item ids change at every login, so items are matched to entries by name: the Nth item
    # called "a gold ring" in this container is the Nth gold-ring entry.  Used by refresh,
    # where every item present should overwrite the entry it belongs to.
    def self.reassign(list, container)
      pool = Store.keys_by_slug(container.name)
      list.map { |obj| [obj, (pool[ItemDB.slug(obj.name)] || []).shift] }
    end

    # A normal scan: anything beyond the number already recorded under that name is new, and
    # with retry_locked the entries nothing could be read off get another go.
    def self.new_and_retries(list, container, retry_locked)
      pool = Store.keys_by_slug(container.name)
      todo = []
      list.group_by { |obj| ItemDB.slug(obj.name) }.each do |slug, objs|
        keys = pool[slug] || []
        objs.drop(keys.size).each { |obj| todo << [obj, nil] }
        next unless retry_locked

        # Items with the same name are interchangeable to us, so pair each entry that needs
        # retrying with any one of them.
        keys.select { |key| Store.retryable?(key) }.each_with_index do |key, i|
          todo << [objs[i], key] if objs[i]
        end
      end
      todo
    end

    # Recall + inspect one item and store it under key.  Always returns the stored entry --
    # an item that couldn't be read is recorded as unreadable with the reason, so `retry`
    # can come back to it and the scan's progress line can say what actually happened.
    def self.examine(obj, container, key)
      book = book_item?(obj)

      # RECALL works on the item where it sits, wherever that is, so the recall itself costs no
      # hand shuffling.  Everything below that returns early is an item there was nothing to
      # read off -- but a book/codex keeps its real detail in ANALYZE, not the loresong, so none
      # of those are terminal for one; we go on to analyze it regardless.
      recall = capture("recall ##{obj.id}", RECALL_START)
      unless book
        return unreadable_stub(obj, container, key, 'no response to recall') if recall.empty?
        if Parser.refused?(recall)
          return unreadable_stub(obj, container, key, "the game refused the recall: #{recall.first}")
        end
        # Nothing to read off an item no bard has sung to yet.
        return locked_stub(obj, container, key, recall) if Parser.locked?(recall)
      end

      # ANALYZE reads a book where it sits, exactly as RECALL does, so a book needs no get/put --
      # its spell-spec detail is all we're after, and books aren't worn, so there's no inspect to
      # do for one either.
      analyze = book ? capture("analyze ##{obj.id}", ANALYZE_START, timeout: 3) : []

      begin
        # INSPECT is the one thing that needs the item in hand; it only reports where a thing is
        # worn, so it's skipped for books.  If we can't pick a worn item up, carry on without it
        # -- worn location falls back to the noun table.
        inspect = []
        if @inspect && !book
          if take(obj, container)
            inspect = capture("inspect ##{obj.id}", INSPECT_START, timeout: 3)
          else
            ItemDB.warn "couldn't pick up #{obj.name} (##{obj.id}) to inspect it"
          end
        end

        entry = Parser.parse(recall, inspect, analyze)
        Parser.infer_worn_location(entry, obj.name, obj.noun)
        entry.merge!(identity(obj, container, key))
        entry['raw'] = raw_text(recall, analyze)
        Store.put(key, ItemDB.ordered_entry(entry))
      ensure
        # Back where it came from before the next item is touched, so an interrupted scan
        # leaves at most one thing in your hand.
        stow(obj, container)
      end
    end

    # Recall is the bulk of an entry's raw text; a book's analyze is appended after it so `show`
    # and the unparsed-line debugging can see both.  Empty captures drop out.
    def self.raw_text(recall, analyze)
      blocks = [recall, analyze].reject { |lines| Array(lines).empty? }
      "#{blocks.map { |lines| lines.join("\n") }.join("\n\n")}\n"
    end

    # The fields every entry carries regardless of what the loresong said.  item_id is the
    # id this login only -- the key is what identifies an item from one session to the next.
    def self.identity(obj, container, key)
      {
        'key'        => key,
        'name'       => obj.name,
        'noun'       => obj.noun,
        'item_id'    => obj.id.to_s,
        'container'  => container.name,
        'character'  => XMLData.name,
        'scanned_at' => Time.now.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
      }
    end

    # Recorded so you can list what still needs a bard.  Store.retryable? treats it as
    # unfinished, so `scan ... retry` tries it again once one has sung to it.
    def self.locked_stub(obj, container, key, recall)
      entry = ItemDB.ordered_entry(
        identity(obj, container, key).merge('status' => 'no-loresong',
                                            'raw' => "#{recall.join("\n")}\n")
      )
      Store.put(key, entry)
    end

    # Nothing could be read off this one.  The reason is kept because "no response to recall"
    # and "couldn't get it out of the locker" call for different fixes.
    def self.unreadable_stub(obj, container, key, reason)
      entry = ItemDB.ordered_entry(
        identity(obj, container, key).merge('status' => 'unreadable', 'reason' => reason)
      )
      Store.put(key, entry)
    end

    def self.capture(command, start_pattern, timeout: 4)
      Lich::Util.issue_command(command, start_pattern, /<prompt/,
                              include_end: false, timeout: timeout,
                              silent: !ItemDB.verbose?, usexml: true, quiet: !ItemDB.verbose?)
                .map { |line| strip_xml(line) }.compact.reject { |l| l.strip.empty? }
    end

    # Only put away what you're already carrying if both hands are genuinely full, and put
    # it back once at the end of the scan rather than per item.
    def self.take(obj, container)
      return true if held?(obj)
      if checkright && checkleft
        empty_hands
        @emptied_hands = true
      end
      fput "get ##{obj.id}"
      waitrt?
      return true if await { held?(obj) }

      # A bare `get #id` doesn't always reach into a container that isn't on your body, so
      # say where the thing is before giving up on it -- "on" for a stand/rack, "in" otherwise.
      fput "get ##{obj.id} #{preposition(container)} ##{container.id}"
      waitrt?
      await { held?(obj) }
    end

    def self.stow(obj, container)
      return true unless held?(obj)
      fput "put ##{obj.id} #{preposition(container)} ##{container.id}"
      waitrt?
      return true if await { !held?(obj) }

      # Left holding something that came out of someone else's furniture is worth shouting
      # about -- walk away now and it leaves the locker with you.
      prep = preposition(container)
      ItemDB.warn "couldn't put #{obj.name} back #{prep} #{container.name} -- it's still in your hand"
      false
    end

    # fput returns as soon as the game sends any line back, but GameObj's hands are filled in
    # by Lich's XML parser thread from the <right>/<left> tags, which arrive with the prompt a
    # moment later.  Checking held? straight after a get therefore reads the hand state from
    # *before* the get and calls a successful pickup a failure -- and then stow sees the same
    # stale "not held" and leaves the item in your hand.  So wait for the state to catch up.
    HAND_TIMEOUT = 3

    def self.await(seconds = HAND_TIMEOUT)
      deadline = Time.now + seconds
      loop do
        return true if yield
        return false if Time.now > deadline

        sleep 0.05
      end
    end

    def self.held?(obj)
      [GameObj.right_hand, GameObj.left_hand].compact.any? { |h| h.id.to_s == obj.id.to_s }
    end

    # Entries recorded in this container with no matching item still in it.  Compared by
    # name and count rather than by id, since ids only last one login: if the db holds three
    # "gold ring" entries here and only two rings are present, the last entry is the stale one.
    def self.stale_keys(container, list)
      present = list.group_by { |obj| ItemDB.slug(obj.name) }
      Store.keys_by_slug(container.name).flat_map { |slug, keys| keys.drop(present[slug].to_a.size) }
    end

    # Entries that say they live in this container but aren't in it any more.
    def self.report_missing(container, list)
      stale = stale_keys(container, list)
      return if stale.empty?
      ItemDB.info "#{stale.size} recorded item#{stale.size == 1 ? ' is' : 's are'} " \
                  "no longer in #{container.name} (moved or sold) -- " \
                  ";itemdb prune #{container.name} to drop them"
    end
  end

  # =============================================================================== Usage
  #
  # The game's own accounting of worn slots, from INVENTORY USAGE.  Read on demand and kept in
  # the db, so a search can say "three of your four neck slots are already taken" without
  # going back to the game for it every time.  Recorded per character: the db is shared, and
  # what Drafix is wearing says nothing about anyone else.

  module Usage
    # A row of the chart:  1/8     6/20     As a pin                         1
    # Non-greedy description so the trailing CODE stays out of it.
    ROW = %r{\A(\d+)/(\d+)\s+(\d+)/(\d+)\s+(\S.*?)\s+(\d+)\z}

    # Either the header or the first row is enough to know the answer has started arriving.
    START = Regexp.union(/FUNC\s+TOTAL\s+WORN\s+LOCATION/i, %r{\d+/\d+\s+\d+/\d+\s+\S})

    def self.stored
      Store.data['slot_usage'].to_h[XMLData.name.to_s]
    end

    def self.rows
      stored.to_h['rows'].to_h
    end

    def self.captured_at
      stored.to_h['captured_at']
    end

    # The limits are the account's, not the game's -- a subscription changes them -- so they can
    # only come from INVENTORY USAGE.  Anything that wants to show them asks for a reading the
    # first time this character has none; after that the stored one stands until `;itemdb usage`
    # refreshes it.  Asked at most once per run, so a character who can't be read (no chart came
    # back) doesn't get a command fired at them by every subsequent search.
    def self.ensure_captured
      return rows unless rows.empty?
      return rows if @asked

      @asked = true
      ItemDB.info 'no worn slot limits recorded for you yet -- reading INVENTORY USAGE'
      capture
      rows
    end

    # Ask the game and record what it says.  Returns the parsed rows, or nil if nothing came
    # back that looked like the chart -- in which case whatever was recorded before is left
    # alone, since a stale reading beats no reading.
    def self.capture
      parsed = parse(Scanner.capture('inventory usage', START, timeout: 5))
      if parsed.empty?
        ItemDB.warn 'INVENTORY USAGE gave nothing I could read -- worn slot usage left as it was'
        return nil
      end
      (Store.data['slot_usage'] ||= {})[XMLData.name.to_s] = {
        'captured_at' => Time.now.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'),
        'rows' => parsed
      }
      Store.save
      parsed
    end

    def self.parse(lines)
      Array(lines).each_with_object({}) do |line, acc|
        next unless (m = line.to_s.strip.match(ROW))

        acc[m[6].to_i] = { 'func_used' => m[1].to_i, 'func_cap' => m[2].to_i,
                           'total_used' => m[3].to_i, 'total_cap' => m[4].to_i,
                           'desc' => m[5].strip }
      end
    end

    # Per body area: how many functional slots it has, how many the game says are in use, and
    # which chart rows went into that.  Nothing is assumed -- an area has a limit only once
    # INVENTORY USAGE has reported every row feeding it, and `known` is what says whether cap
    # and used mean anything.  Each row carries what the game said about it, so a summary can
    # show which limits it added together.
    def self.by_slot
      captured = rows
      ItemDB::WORN_SLOTS.each_with_object({}) do |row, acc|
        area = acc[row[:slot]] ||= { 'used' => 0, 'cap' => 0, 'known' => true, 'rows' => [] }
        # YAML round-trips integer keys, but a db hand-edited into string keys shouldn't break.
        got = captured[row[:code]] || captured[row[:code].to_s]
        area['rows'] << row.merge(cap: got && got['func_cap'].to_i,
                                  used: got && got['func_used'].to_i,
                                  desc: (got && got['desc'].to_s.downcase) || row[:desc])
        if got
          area['cap']  += got['func_cap'].to_i
          area['used'] += got['func_used'].to_i
        else
          area['known'] = false
        end
      end
    end

    # Rows the game reported that WORN_SLOTS has no entry for -- a new worn slot, or a chart
    # that differs from the one this was written against.  Keyed by code.
    def self.unknown_rows
      known = ItemDB::WORN_SLOTS.map { |r| r[:code] }
      rows.reject { |code, _row| known.include?(code.to_i) }
    end

    # The chart itself, in code order, merged with whatever the game last said about each row.
    # A row the game hasn't reported has no limit -- that number is the account's, not ours.
    def self.chart
      captured = rows
      ItemDB::WORN_SLOTS.map do |row|
        got = captured[row[:code]] || captured[row[:code].to_s]
        row.merge(used: got && got['func_used'].to_i,
                  cap: got && got['func_cap'].to_i,
                  desc: (got && got['desc'].to_s.downcase) || row[:desc])
      end
    end
  end

  # =============================================================================== Query
  #
  # A query is a boolean expression over terms.  `and` (also `,` / `&`) and `or` (also `|`)
  # join terms, `!` / `not` negates, and parentheses group -- so  neck & (dex | ranged)  is
  # neckworn AND (boosts Dexterity OR boosts Ranged).  Each term is either one of the SPECIALS
  # below or a boost term: <name> [base|bonus|ranks] [op value].
  #
  # tokenize turns the text into a flat token stream; Parser is a recursive-descent parse of
  # that stream into a single lambda(item) -> bool, with the usual precedence (`not` tighter
  # than `and` tighter than `or`).

  module Query
    OPS = %w[>= <= != <> == > < =].freeze

    class Error < StandardError; end

    def self.matches(query)
      test = parse(query)
      Store.items.select { |_id, item| item.is_a?(Hash) && test.call(item) }
    end

    # Text -> a single predicate.  Raises Query::Error on an empty or malformed query.
    def self.parse(query)
      tokens = tokenize(query)
      raise Error, 'empty query' if tokens.empty?

      parser = Parser.new(tokens)
      test   = parser.expression
      raise Error, "unexpected '#{parser.peek_text}'" unless parser.eof?
      test
    end

    # Breaks the query into [:lparen] [:rparen] [:and] [:or] [:not] and [:term, text] tokens.
    #
    # The boolean punctuation ( ) | & , is space-padded so it always tokenizes on its own; the
    # comparison operators (> < = ! <> >= <= == !=) are left glued to their term, so "int>2"
    # and "agi bonus != 5" stay whole.  A leading `!` on a term is negation and becomes its own
    # token, while a `!=` in the middle stays part of the term.  "stalking and hiding" is one
    # skill name, so its internal "and" is squashed away before anything else looks at it.
    #
    # Consecutive word tokens are NOT merged here -- Parser#atom joins them -- so "intuition
    # bonus" stays two adjacent [:term] tokens that become one term, while "int and agi" has an
    # [:and] between them and stays two terms.
    def self.tokenize(query)
      s = query.to_s.downcase.gsub(/stalking\s+and\s+hiding/, 'stalkingandhiding')
      s = s.gsub(/([(),|&])/, ' \1 ')
      s.split(/\s+/).reject(&:empty?).flat_map do |raw|
        case raw
        when '('           then [[:lparen]]
        when ')'           then [[:rparen]]
        when '|', 'or'     then [[:or]]
        when '&', ',', 'and' then [[:and]]
        when '!', 'not'    then [[:not]]
        when /\A!(?!=)(.+)\z/ then [[:not], [:term, Regexp.last_match(1)]]  # glued !term
        else [[:term, raw]]
        end
      end
    end

    # Returns a lambda(item) -> bool for a single already-assembled term.  Negation and grouping
    # are the Parser's job, so a term here is just  <name> [base|bonus|ranks] [op value]  or one
    # of the specials.
    def self.compile(term)
      test = special(term) || boost(term)
      raise Error, "don't know how to search for '#{term}'" unless test
      test
    end

    # Recursive descent over the token list from tokenize.  Each method returns a
    # lambda(item) -> bool, so the whole expression compiles down to one predicate.
    #
    #   expression := or_expr
    #   or_expr    := and_expr (OR and_expr)*
    #   and_expr   := not_expr ((AND | <adjacency>) not_expr)*
    #   not_expr   := NOT not_expr | atom
    #   atom       := LPAREN expression RPAREN | TERM+
    #
    # Two atoms sitting next to each other with no joiner between them (e.g. `neck (dex|ranged)`)
    # are ANDed -- but plain adjacent words are gathered into one term by #atom, so only a paren
    # or a negation can start a second atom without an explicit `and`.
    class Parser
      def initialize(tokens)
        @tokens = tokens
        @pos    = 0
      end

      def peek
        @tokens[@pos]
      end

      def peek_type
        (t = peek) && t[0]
      end

      # A readable name for whatever token is next, for error messages.
      TOKEN_TEXT = { lparen: '(', rparen: ')', and: 'and', or: 'or', not: 'not' }.freeze

      def peek_text
        t = peek
        return 'end of query' unless t
        t[0] == :term ? t[1] : TOKEN_TEXT[t[0]]
      end

      def advance
        t = @tokens[@pos]
        @pos += 1
        t
      end

      def eof?
        @pos >= @tokens.length
      end

      def expression
        or_expr
      end

      def or_expr
        parts = [and_expr]
        while peek_type == :or
          advance
          parts << and_expr
        end
        parts.size == 1 ? parts.first : ->(item) { parts.any? { |p| p.call(item) } }
      end

      def and_expr
        parts = [not_expr]
        loop do
          if peek_type == :and
            advance
            parts << not_expr
          elsif atom_start?         # adjacent atom, no explicit joiner -> implicit AND
            parts << not_expr
          else
            break
          end
        end
        parts.size == 1 ? parts.first : ->(item) { parts.all? { |p| p.call(item) } }
      end

      def not_expr
        return atom unless peek_type == :not

        advance
        inner = not_expr
        ->(item) { !inner.call(item) }
      end

      def atom
        case peek_type
        when :lparen
          advance
          node = or_expr
          raise Error, 'missing )' unless peek_type == :rparen
          advance
          node
        when :term
          words = []
          words << advance[1] while peek_type == :term
          Query.compile(words.join(' '))
        when nil
          raise Error, 'query ended early'
        else
          raise Error, "unexpected '#{peek_text}'"
        end
      end

      # What can begin an atom -- used to spot two atoms with no joiner between them.
      def atom_start?
        %i[lparen term not].include?(peek_type)
      end
    end

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ non-boost terms

    def self.special(term)
      case term
      # enhancive / magical fate.  Qualified forms first; bare `persist` and `crumbly`
      # mean the enhancive side, since that's what this db is mostly for.
      when /\A(?:enh|enhancive)\s+(?:crumbl\w*|dust)\z/, /\Acrumbl\w*\z/
        ->(i) { i.dig('enhancive', 'after_last_charge') == 'crumble' }
      when /\A(?:enh|enhancive)\s+persist\w*\z/, /\Apersist\w*\z/
        ->(i) { i.dig('enhancive', 'after_last_charge') == 'persist' }
      when /\Amagic(?:al)?\s+persist\w*\z/
        ->(i) { i.dig('magic', 'after_last_charge') == 'persist' }
      when /\Amagic(?:al)?\s+(?:crumbl\w*|destroy\w*)\z/, /\A(?:magic(?:al)?\s+)?disintegrat\w*\z/
        ->(i) { i.dig('magic', 'after_last_charge') == 'disintegrate' }
      when /\A(?:enh|enhancive)\z/
        ->(i) { !i['enhancive'].nil? }

      # imbedded spells
      when /\A(?:imbedded|embedded|spell)\z/
        ->(i) { !i.dig('magic', 'spell').nil? }
      when /\Aspell[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i.dig('magic', 'spell').to_s.downcase.include?(want) }
      when /\Amagic(?:al)?\z/
        ->(i) { !i['magic'].nil? }
      when /\A(?:act|activator)[:=]\s*(\w+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i.dig('magic', 'activator').to_s.start_with?(want) }

      # spell-specialization books:  spec for any, spec:<spell|number> to name one, specranks>=N
      # for how many ranks it grants.
      when /\A(?:spec|specialization|specranks?)\s*(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(\d+)\z/
        op, val = Regexp.last_match(1), Regexp.last_match(2).to_i
        ->(i) { (r = i.dig('spell_spec', 'ranks')) && Query.compare(r, op, val) }
      when /\A(?:spec|specialization|spellspec)[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1).strip
        lambda do |i|
          ss = i['spell_spec']
          next false unless ss
          ss['spell'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) || ss['number'].to_s == want
        end
      when /\A(?:spec|specialization|spellspec)\z/
        ->(i) { !i['spell_spec'].nil? }

      # flares and enchant
      when /\Aflares?\z/
        ->(i) { !Array(i['flares']).empty? }
      when /\Atemp(?:orary)?\s+flares?\z/
        ->(i) { ItemDB.flares(i).any? { |f| f['temporary'] } }
      when /\Aflares?[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { ItemDB.flares(i).any? { |f| "#{f['type']} #{f['message']}".downcase.include?(want) } }
      when /\Amaxench\w*\s*(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(\d+)\z/
        op, val = Regexp.last_match(1), Regexp.last_match(2).to_i
        ->(i) { i['max_enchant'] && Query.compare(i['max_enchant'], op, val) }
      when /\Amaxench\w*\z/
        ->(i) { !i['max_enchant'].nil? }
      when /\A(?:enchanter|enchantedby)[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i['enchanted_by'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) }
      when /\Aenchant\w*\s*(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(\d+)\z/
        op, val = Regexp.last_match(1), Regexp.last_match(2).to_i
        ->(i) { i['enchant'] && Query.compare(i['enchant'], op, val) }
      when /\Aenchant\w*\z/
        ->(i) { !i['enchant'].nil? }

      # numbers
      when /\Aworth\s*(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*([\d.,]+[km]?)\z/
        op = Regexp.last_match(1)
        val = ItemDB.parse_silver(Regexp.last_match(2))
        ->(i) { i['worth'] && Query.compare(i['worth'], op, val) }
      # "charges" without a qualifier means either kind; echarges / mcharges pick one.
      when /\A(e|enh|enhancive\s*|m|mag|magic(?:al)?\s*)?charges?\s*(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(\d+)\z/
        which = Regexp.last_match(1).to_s[0]
        op, val = Regexp.last_match(2), Regexp.last_match(3).to_i
        lambda do |i|
          enh = which != 'm' && Query.charge_match?(i, op, val)
          mag = which != 'e' && (c = i.dig('magic', 'charges')) && Query.compare(c, op, val)
          !!(enh || mag)
        end
      when /\Acharges?[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i.dig('enhancive', 'charges_phrase').to_s.include?(want) }
      when /\A(?:trained|train|level|lvl)\s*(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(\d+)\z/
        op, val = Regexp.last_match(1), Regexp.last_match(2).to_i
        ->(i) { (r = Query.highest_requirement(i)) && Query.compare(r, op, val) }

      # where / who / what.  `ear` is one ear, `2ear` both -- separate slots, separate limits.
      when /\Aworn[:=]\s*(\w+)\z/
        want = ItemDB.canonical_slot(Regexp.last_match(1))
        ->(i) { i['worn_location'] == want }
      when /\A(#{ItemDB::SLOT_QUERY_WORDS.join('|')})\z/
        want = ItemDB.canonical_slot(Regexp.last_match(1))
        ->(i) { i['worn_location'] == want }
      when /\A(?:container|cont|in)[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i['container'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) }
      when /\A(?:char|character|who)[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i['character'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) }
      when /\A(?:material|mat)[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i['material'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) }
      when /\Aname[:=]\s*(.+)\z/
        want = Regexp.last_match(1)
        ->(i) { i['name'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) }

      # damage resistance -- resist:fire, resist:nature>=50, or bare resist for any at all.
      # (`resistant` on its own stays the magic-resistant flag, below.)
      when /\Aresist(?:ance|ant)?[:=]\s*(\w+)\s*(?:(#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(\d+))?\z/
        type = ItemDB.normalize_resist(Regexp.last_match(1))
        op, val = Regexp.last_match(2), Regexp.last_match(3)&.to_i
        lambda do |i|
          pct = i.dig('resistances', type)
          next false if pct.nil?
          op ? Query.compare(pct, op, val) : true
        end
      when /\Aresist(?:ance|ances)?\z/
        ->(i) { !(i['resistances'] || {}).empty? }

      # flags
      when /\Aholy\z/      then ->(i) { i.dig('flags', 'holy') == true }
      when /\Aunholy\z/    then ->(i) { i.dig('flags', 'unholy') == true }
      when /\A(?:magic\s+)?resistant\z/ then ->(i) { i.dig('flags', 'magic_resistant') == true }
      # padded/weighted/sighted hold a degree word now, so match on presence, not `== true`.
      when /\Apadd\w*\z/   then ->(i) { !i.dig('flags', 'padded').nil? }
      when /\Aweight\w*\z/ then ->(i) { !i.dig('flags', 'weighted').nil? }
      when /\Asight\w*\z/  then ->(i) { !i.dig('flags', 'sighted').nil? }
      when /\Aensorcell\w*\z/ then ->(i) { !i.dig('flags', 'ensorcelled').nil? }
      when /\Asanctif\w*\z/   then ->(i) { !i.dig('flags', 'sanctified').nil? }
      when /\Aalter\w*\z/  then ->(i) { !i.dig('flags', 'alter_difficulty').nil? }
      when /\Aunreadable\z/ then ->(i) { i['status'] == 'unreadable' }
      when /\A(?:no\s*loresong|locked|unsung|needs?\s*a?\s*bard)\z/
        ->(i) { i['status'] == 'no-loresong' }
      end
    end

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------- boost terms

    def self.boost(term)
      m = term.match(/\A(?<name>.+?)\s*(?:\b(?<type>base|bonus|bonuses|rank|ranks)\b)?\s*
                      (?:(?<op>#{OPS.join('|')})\s*(?<val>\d+))?\z/x)
      return nil unless m && !m[:name].to_s.strip.empty?

      names = resolve(m[:name])
      raise Error, "unknown skill or stat '#{m[:name].strip}'" if names.nil?

      type = m[:type] && m[:type].sub(/\Abonuses\z/, 'bonus').sub(/\Arank\z/, 'ranks')
      op   = m[:op]
      val  = m[:val] && m[:val].to_i

      lambda do |item|
        found = Array(item.dig('enhancive', 'boosts')).select { |b| names.include?(b['name']) }
        found = found.select { |b| b['type'] == type } if type
        next false if found.empty?
        next true  unless op
        found.any? { |b| Query.compare(b['value'], op, val) }
      end
    end

    # Abbreviation first, then loose substring over the boost names actually in the db.
    # nil means "no idea what that is" so the caller can complain instead of returning
    # a confidently empty result.
    def self.resolve(raw)
      key = ItemDB.squash(raw)
      return [] if key.empty?
      return [ItemDB::ALIASES[key]] if ItemDB::ALIASES[key]

      universe = Store.boost_names + ItemDB::STAT_NAMES + ItemDB::ALIASES.values
      universe = universe.uniq

      exact = universe.select { |n| ItemDB.squash(n) == key }
      return exact unless exact.empty?

      loose = universe.select { |n| ItemDB.squash(n).include?(key) }
      loose.empty? ? nil : loose
    end

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------- helpers

    def self.compare(actual, op, val)
      return false if actual.nil?
      case op
      when '>'        then actual > val
      when '<'        then actual < val
      when '>='       then actual >= val
      when '<='       then actual <= val
      when '=', '=='  then actual == val
      when '!=', '<>' then actual != val
      else false
      end
    end

    # Charges are a range, not a number, so a comparison matches when the range overlaps
    # the values that would satisfy it.  Deliberately generous -- better a maybe than a
    # silently hidden candidate.
    def self.charge_match?(item, op, val)
      min = item.dig('enhancive', 'charges_min')
      max = item.dig('enhancive', 'charges_max')
      return false if min.nil?
      max ||= Float::INFINITY
      case op
      when '>'        then max > val
      when '>='       then max >= val
      when '<'        then min < val
      when '<='       then min <= val
      when '=', '=='  then min <= val && val <= max
      when '!=', '<>' then !(min == val && max == val)
      else false
      end
    end

    def self.highest_requirement(item)
      reqs = Array(item.dig('enhancive', 'boosts')).map { |b| b['min_trained'] }.compact
      reqs.max
    end
  end

  # ============================================================================= Display

  module Display
    # No item id here on purpose: the game reassigns ids every login, so one written down
    # yesterday points at nothing (or worse, at something else) today.  key: true adds the
    # db key, which is stable, for the times you have to name one entry out of several.
    def self.line(item, key: false)
      bits = []
      # Not truncated: the key is what you have to type back at it.
      bits << format('%-24s', item['key'].to_s) if key
      bits << format('%-46s', item['name'].to_s[0, 46])
      bits << format('%-9s', item['worn_location'] || '-')
      bits << format('%7s', ItemDB.short_silver(item['worth']))
      bits.join(' ')
    end

    # Training requirements are left to `show` -- in a list they're noise.
    def self.boosts(item)
      Array(item.dig('enhancive', 'boosts')).map do |b|
        name = [b['name'], b['type'] && b['type'].capitalize].compact.join(' ')
        "#{name} +#{b['value']}"
      end
    end

    def self.enhancive_summary(item)
      enh = item['enhancive']
      return nil unless enh
      chg = enh['charges_phrase']
      est = if enh['charges_min']
              enh['charges_max'] ? "~#{enh['charges_min']}-#{enh['charges_max']}" : "~#{enh['charges_min']}+"
            end
      ["enh:", [chg, est && "(#{est})"].compact.join(' '),
       enh['after_last_charge'] == 'persist' ? '- persists' : '- crumbles'].compact.join(' ')
    end

    def self.magic_summary(item)
      mag = item['magic']
      return nil unless mag
      parts = []
      parts << (mag['spell'] || 'no spell')
      parts << "#{mag['charges']} chg" unless mag['charges'].nil?
      parts << "max #{mag['max_recharge']}" if mag['max_recharge']
      parts << 'rechargeable' if mag['rechargeable']
      parts << mag['activator'] if mag['activator']
      parts << (mag['after_last_charge'] == 'persist' ? 'persists' : 'disintegrates') if mag['after_last_charge']
      "magic: #{parts.join(', ')}"
    end

    # A one-liner for a spell-specialization book:  the spell it specializes, its number, and how
    # many ranks READing it grants.  nil for anything that isn't one, so callers can .compact.
    def self.spell_spec_summary(item)
      ss = item['spell_spec']
      return nil unless ss
      spell = [ss['spell'], ss['number'] && "(#{ss['number']})"].compact.join(' ')
      "spell specialization: +#{ss['ranks']} rank#{'s' if ss['ranks'] != 1} to #{spell} (consumed on READ)"
    end

    # "padded" + the stored degree word => "padded (very heavily)"; a bare `true` (no degree
    # captured) renders just the word.  nil for an absent treatment so callers can `.compact`.
    def self.treatment(word, val)
      return nil if val.nil?
      val == true ? word : "#{word} (#{val})"
    end

    def self.extras(item)
      flags = item['flags'] || {}
      parts = []
      if item['enchant']
        parts << "enchant +#{item['enchant']}#{" (max #{item['max_enchant']})" if item['max_enchant']}"
      elsif item['max_enchant']
        parts << "max enchant #{item['max_enchant']}"
      end
      parts << "flares: #{ItemDB.flare_labels(item).join(', ')}" unless Array(item['flares']).empty?
      unless (res = item['resistances'] || {}).empty?
        parts << "resist: #{res.map { |t, p| "#{t} #{p}%" }.join(', ')}"
      end
      parts << item['material'] if item['material']
      parts << 'holy' if flags['holy']
      parts << 'unholy' if flags['unholy']
      parts << 'magic resistant' if flags['magic_resistant']
      parts << treatment('padded', flags['padded'])
      parts << treatment('weighted', flags['weighted'])
      parts << treatment('sighted', flags['sighted'])
      parts << "ensorcelled #{flags['ensorcelled']}x" if flags['ensorcelled']
      parts << "sanctified #{flags['sanctified']}x" if flags['sanctified']
      parts.compact!
      parts.empty? ? nil : parts.join('  ')
    end

    # The block used by search results and `list`.
    def self.block(item)
      out = [line(item)]
      case item['status']
      when 'unreadable'
        out << "    (#{item['reason'] || 'no response to recall'} -- scan again with `retry`)"
      when 'no-loresong' then out << '    (no loresong yet -- a bard has to unlock it)'
      end
      bs = boosts(item)
      out.concat(wrap(bs, 3).map { |row| "    #{row}" }) unless bs.empty?
      tail = [enhancive_summary(item), magic_summary(item)].compact.join('   ')
      out << "    #{tail}" unless tail.empty?
      ss = spell_spec_summary(item)
      out << "    #{ss}" if ss
      ex = extras(item)
      out << "    #{ex}" if ex
      out.join("\n")
    end

    def self.detail(item)
      out = []
      out << "#{item['name']}  [#{item['key']}]"
      out << "  noun            #{item['noun']}"
      out << "  status          #{item['status']}#{" (#{item['reason']})" if item['reason']}" if item['status'] != 'ok'
      out << "  worn            #{item['worn_location'] || 'unknown'}" \
             "#{" (#{item['worn_source']}#{": #{item['worn_phrase']}" if item['worn_phrase']})" if item['worn_source']}"
      out << "  worth           #{item['worth'] ? "#{ItemDB.commify(item['worth'])} silvers" : 'unknown'}"
      out << "  weight          #{item['weight_desc'] || 'unknown'}"
      out << "  material        #{item['material']}" if item['material']
      out << "  enchant         +#{item['enchant']} more than usual" if item['enchant']
      out << "  max enchant     +#{item['max_enchant']}" if item['max_enchant']
      out << "  enchanted by    #{item['enchanted_by']}" if item['enchanted_by']
      ItemDB.flares(item).each_with_index do |f, idx|
        out << format('  %-15s %s', idx.zero? ? 'flares' : '',
                      "#{f['type']}#{' (temporary)' if f['temporary']} -- #{f['message']}")
      end
      (item['resistances'] || {}).each_with_index do |(type, pct), idx|
        out << format('  %-15s %s', idx.zero? ? 'resistance' : '', "#{pct}% to #{type}")
      end

      flags = item['flags'] || {}
      # padding / weighting / sighting: expand the degree word into its CER band and cost.
      [%w[padded padding_temporary], %w[weighted weighting_temporary],
       %w[sighted sighting_temporary]].each do |key, tkey|
        next unless flags[key]
        detail = flags[key] == true ? key : "#{key} -- #{ItemDB.wps_summary(flags[key])}"
        detail += ' (temporary)' if flags[tkey]
        out << format('  %-15s %s', 'treatment', detail)
      end
      out << "  ensorcelled     #{flags['ensorcelled']} times" if flags['ensorcelled']
      out << "  sanctified      #{flags['sanctified']} times" if flags['sanctified']
      out << "  complexity      #{flags['alter_class']} (#{flags['alter_difficulty']} difficulty)" if flags['alter_difficulty']
      out << '  holy            yes' if flags['holy']
      out << '  unholy          yes' if flags['unholy']
      out << '  magic resistant yes' if flags['magic_resistant']

      out << "  loresong        #{item['loresong_by']}" if item['loresong_by']

      if (mag = item['magic'])
        out << '  magic'
        out << "    spell         #{mag['spell'] || '(none imbedded)'}"
        out << "    charges       #{mag['charges']}#{" of #{mag['max_recharge']} max" if mag['max_recharge']}"
        out << "    rechargeable  #{mag['rechargeable'] ? 'yes' : 'no'}"
        out << "    activator     #{mag['activator_phrase'] || mag['activator']}"
        out << "    last charge   #{mag['after_last_charge'] == 'persist' ? 'persists' : 'disintegrates'}"
      end

      if (enh = item['enhancive'])
        out << '  enhancive'
        out << "    charges       #{enh['charges_phrase']}" \
               "#{" (tier #{enh['charges_tier']}, est #{enh['charges_min']}-#{enh['charges_max'] || '+'})" if enh['charges_tier']}"
        out << "    last charge   #{enh['after_last_charge'] == 'persist' ? 'persists' : 'crumbles into dust'}"
        Array(enh['boosts']).each do |b|
          out << format('    %-11s %-32s +%-3d %s', b['category'],
                        [b['name'], b['type'] && b['type'].capitalize].compact.join(' '),
                        b['value'],
                        [b['min_trained'] && "needs #{b['min_trained']} trainings",
                         b['base_equiv'] && b['type'] == 'bonus' && "= #{b['base_equiv']} base",
                         b['cap'] && "cap #{b['cap']}"].select { |x| x }.join(', '))
        end
      end

      if (ss = item['spell_spec'])
        out << '  spell specialization'
        out << "    spell         #{ss['spell']}#{" (#{ss['number']})" if ss['number']}"
        out << "    adds          +#{ss['ranks']} specialization rank#{'s' if ss['ranks'] != 1} when READ"
        out << '    one-time use  READing the book consumes it'
      end

      out << "  container       #{item['container']}"
      out << "  character       #{item['character']}"
      out << "  scanned         #{item['scanned_at']}"
      out << "  unparsed        #{item['unrecognized'].join(' | ')}" if item['unrecognized']
      out.join("\n")
    end

    # The stat/skill/resource tables under `totals` and under a search's results are the same
    # tables -- the only difference is which items were added up to make them.
    def self.boost_totals(agg)
      stats, skills, resources = agg.values_at('stats', 'skills', 'resources')
      out = []
      unless stats.empty?
        out << "stats (cap #{ItemDB::STAT_CAP} base-equivalent; 1 bonus = 2 base)"
        stats.sort_by { |n, _| n }.each do |name, v|
          equiv = v['base'] + v['bonus'] * 2
          out << format('  %-14s base %-4d bonus %-4d = %d base-equiv%s',
                        name, v['base'], v['bonus'], equiv,
                        equiv > ItemDB::STAT_CAP ? "  (over the #{ItemDB::STAT_CAP} cap)" : '')
        end
      end
      unless skills.empty?
        out << '' unless out.empty?
        out << "skills (cap #{ItemDB::SKILL_CAP})"
        skills.sort_by { |n, _| n }.each do |name, v|
          total = v['ranks'] + v['bonus']
          out << format('  %-30s ranks %-4d bonus %-4d = %d%s',
                        name, v['ranks'], v['bonus'], total,
                        total > ItemDB::SKILL_CAP ? "  (over the #{ItemDB::SKILL_CAP} cap)" : '')
        end
      end
      unless resources.empty?
        out << '' unless out.empty?
        out << 'resources'
        resources.sort_by { |n, _| n }.each { |name, v| out << format('  %-20s %d', name, v) }
      end
      out
    end

    # Which worn slots a set of items is competing for, against what the game says is already
    # taken.  `label` names the count column -- a search counts what it matched, totals counts
    # the whole db.  `free` is what the game leaves you, `after` is what would be left if every
    # item in the count column went on; a negative number there is the whole point of the table.
    # Both only mean something once INVENTORY USAGE has been read, so before that they're dashes
    # and only the limits are shown.
    def self.slot_usage(items, label)
      wanted = {}
      items.each do |item|
        slot = item['worn_location'] || 'unknown'
        wanted[slot] = wanted.fetch(slot, 0) + 1
      end
      return [] if wanted.empty?

      areas = Usage.by_slot
      out = ['worn slots, functional only -- ' +
             (Usage.captured_at ? "limits and usage as the game reported them #{Usage.captured_at}; after = free once these are on" : 'run ;itemdb usage to read your limits and what you have on')]
      out << format('  %-9s %8s %7s %6s %5s %6s', 'slot', label, 'in use', 'limit', 'free', 'after')
      # Items whose worn location was never read go last -- they're a gap in the db, not a
      # body area competing with the others.
      wanted.sort_by { |slot, count| [slot == 'unknown' ? 1 : 0, -count, slot] }.each do |slot, count|
        area = areas[slot]
        known = area && area['known']
        free = known ? area['cap'] - area['used'] : nil
        out << format('  %-9s %8d %7s %6s %5s %6s%s', slot, count,
                      known ? area['used'] : '-', known ? area['cap'] : '-',
                      free || '-', free ? free - count : '-',
                      slot_note(slot, area))
      end
      out
    end

    # What a row of numbers can't say for itself: why a slot has no limit to compare against,
    # and which chart rows were added together to reach the one it does have.
    def self.slot_note(slot, area)
      note = if slot == 'unknown'
               'worn location never read -- a scan without `quick` inspects for it'
             elsif !area
               'no INVENTORY USAGE row names this, so no limit to give you'
             elsif !area['known'] && !Usage.rows.empty?
               'the last INVENTORY USAGE left out ' +
                 area['rows'].reject { |r| r[:cap] }.map { |r| "##{r[:code]} #{r[:desc]}" }.join(', ')
             elsif area['rows'].size > 1
               area['rows'].map { |r| [r[:cap], r[:desc]].compact.join(' ') }.join(' + ')
             end
      note ? "   (#{note})" : ''
    end

    # Pack short strings into rows of n columns, each column only as wide as it needs.
    def self.wrap(strings, per_row)
      rows = strings.each_slice(per_row).to_a
      widths = (0...per_row).map { |col| rows.map { |r| r[col].to_s.length }.max + 2 }
      rows.map { |row| row.each_with_index.map { |s, i| s.ljust(widths[i]) }.join.rstrip }
    end
  end

  # ================================================================================= CLI

  def self.verbose?
    @verbose ||= false
  end

  def self.info(msg)
    Lich::Messaging.msg('info', "itemdb: #{msg}")
  end

  def self.warn(msg)
    Lich::Messaging.msg('warn', "itemdb: #{msg}")
  end

  def self.mono(msg)
    Lich::Messaging.mono(msg, encode: true)
  end

  # One line per item as the scan walks the container, whatever the outcome.  Items that
  # can't be read produce no other output, so without this a run of them is indistinguishable
  # from the script having hung.
  def self.progress(index, total, obj, note)
    info "[#{index}/#{total}] #{obj.name} -- #{note}"
  end

  def self.boost_summary(entry)
    boosts = Display.boosts(entry)
    return boosts.join(', ') unless boosts.empty?
    return Display.spell_spec_summary(entry) if entry['spell_spec']

    entry['enhancive'] ? 'enhancive, no boosts parsed' : 'not enhancive'
  end

  # Adds the enhancive boosts of a set of entries together, split the three ways the caps work:
  # stats have separate base and bonus columns (a bonus point is worth two base), skills have
  # ranks and bonus, and everything else (Max Mana, Stamina Recovery, ...) is a flat number.
  # Shared by `totals`, which adds up the whole db, and by a search, which adds up what it
  # found -- so the two can never disagree about what a pile of items comes to.
  def self.aggregate_boosts(items)
    stats = {}
    skills = {}
    resources = {}
    items.each do |item|
      Array(item.dig('enhancive', 'boosts')).each do |b|
        case b['category']
        when 'stat'
          s = stats[b['name']] ||= { 'base' => 0, 'bonus' => 0 }
          s[b['type']] += b['value'] if s.key?(b['type'])
        when 'skill'
          s = skills[b['name']] ||= { 'base' => 0, 'bonus' => 0, 'ranks' => 0 }
          s[b['type']] += b['value'] if s.key?(b['type'])
        else
          resources[b['name']] = resources.fetch(b['name'], 0) + b['value']
        end
      end
    end
    { 'stats' => stats, 'skills' => skills, 'resources' => resources }
  end

  # Keeps the YAML in a predictable, readable order with the bulky bits last.
  KEY_ORDER = %w[key name noun status reason worn_location worn_phrase worn_source worth weight_lbs
                 weight_desc enchant max_enchant enchanted_by flares resistances material
                 loresong_by magic enhancive spell_spec flags container character item_id scanned_at
                 unrecognized raw].freeze

  def self.ordered_entry(entry)
    ordered = {}
    KEY_ORDER.each { |k| ordered[k] = entry[k] if entry.key?(k) }
    entry.each { |k, v| ordered[k] = v unless ordered.key?(k) }
    # Drop keys we never learned anything about so the YAML stays skimmable.
    ordered.reject { |_k, v| v.nil? || v == [] || v == {} }
  end

  # Find db entries by key or by a name keyword.  Never by item id: those are handed out
  # fresh at every login, so a number noted down earlier may now belong to another item.
  def self.lookup(needle)
    needle = needle.to_s.sub(/\A#/, '').strip
    return [] if needle.empty?
    return [[needle, Store.items[needle]]] if Store.items.key?(needle)

    want = needle.downcase
    Store.items.select { |key, e| e['name'].to_s.downcase.include?(want) || key.include?(want) }.to_a
  end

  # A bare number is almost certainly an item id someone copied out of the game.
  def self.id_needle?(text)
    text.to_s.strip =~ /\A#?\d+\z/
  end

  def self.warn_id_needle(text, advice)
    warn "item ids change every login, so ##{text.to_s.strip.sub(/\A#/, '')} means nothing " \
         "to the db -- #{advice}"
  end

  def self.default_container
    Store.data['last_container'] || 'backpack'
  end

  def self.main(args)
    dispatch(args)
    # A 1.0.0 database converted during load only reaches disk if something writes it back,
    # and a read-only command otherwise wouldn't.
    Store.save if Store.migrated?
  end

  def self.dispatch(args)
    args = args.to_s.strip
    return help if args.empty? || args =~ /\A(help|\?|-h|--help)\z/i

    verb, rest = args.split(/\s+/, 2)
    rest = rest.to_s.strip

    case verb.downcase
    when 'scan'    then cmd_scan(rest, refresh: false)
    when 'refresh' then cmd_refresh(rest)
    when 'remove', 'rm', 'delete' then cmd_remove(rest)
    when 'prune'   then cmd_prune(rest)
    when 'clear'   then cmd_clear(rest)
    when 'list'    then cmd_list(rest)
    when 'show'    then cmd_show(rest)
    when 'totals', 'caps' then cmd_totals
    when 'usage', 'slots' then cmd_usage(rest)
    when 'verbose' then @verbose = !verbose?; info "verbose recall output #{verbose? ? 'on' : 'off'}"
    else cmd_search(args)
    end
  end

  # Words you can tack onto a scan:  quick (don't hold items to inspect them) and
  # retry (also re-check entries nothing could be read off last time).
  MODIFIERS = {
    quick: /(?:\A|\s)(?:quick|noinspect|fast)\z/i,
    retry: /(?:\A|\s)(?:retry|recheck)\z/i
  }.freeze

  def self.strip_modifiers(rest)
    text = rest.to_s.strip
    mods = {}
    while (hit = MODIFIERS.find { |_name, pattern| text =~ pattern })
      mods[hit[0]] = true
      text = text.sub(hit[1], '').strip
    end
    [text, mods]
  end

  def self.cmd_scan(rest, refresh: false)
    container, mods = strip_modifiers(rest)
    container = default_container if container.empty?
    Scanner.run(container, refresh: refresh,
                inspect_items: !mods[:quick], retry_locked: !!mods[:retry])
  end

  # With no argument, or with a container name, this re-reads the whole container.  Anything
  # else is taken as an item keyword and only the matching items are recalled again.
  def self.cmd_refresh(rest)
    text, mods = strip_modifiers(rest)
    return cmd_scan(rest, refresh: true) if text.empty? || Scanner.find_container(text)

    if id_needle?(text)
      warn_id_needle(text, 'refresh by item name instead')
      return
    end

    entries = lookup(text).map(&:last)
    if entries.empty?
      warn "'#{text}' is neither a container you have open nor an item in the db"
      return
    end
    containers = entries.map { |e| e['container'] }.compact.uniq
    if containers.size > 1
      warn "'#{text}' matches items in #{containers.join(', ')} -- refresh one container at a time"
      return
    end
    Scanner.run(containers.first || default_container, refresh: true,
                only_name: text, inspect_items: !mods[:quick])
  end

  def self.cmd_remove(rest)
    if rest.empty?
      warn 'usage: ;itemdb remove <name keyword|key>'
      return
    end
    if id_needle?(rest)
      warn_id_needle(rest, 'remove by item name instead')
      return
    end
    found = lookup(rest)
    if found.empty?
      warn "nothing in the db matches '#{rest}'"
    elsif found.size > 1
      warn "'#{rest}' matches #{found.size} items -- remove one by its key (first column):"
      mono found.map { |_key, e| Display.line(e, key: true) }.join("\n")
    else
      key, entry = found.first
      Store.delete(key)
      Store.save
      info "removed #{entry['name']} [#{key}]"
    end
  end

  def self.cmd_prune(container)
    container = default_container if container.empty?
    obj = Scanner.find_container(container)
    unless obj
      warn "no container matching '#{container}' -- in reach: " \
           "#{Scanner.containers_in_reach.map(&:name).join(', ')}"
      return
    end
    list = Scanner.contents(obj)
    if list.nil?
      warn "#{obj.name} looks closed -- open it so I can see what's really inside"
      return
    end
    stale = Scanner.stale_keys(obj, list)
    if stale.empty?
      info "nothing stale recorded for #{obj.name}"
      return
    end
    dropped = stale.map { |key| Store.items[key] }.compact
    stale.each { |key| Store.delete(key) }
    Store.save
    info "pruned #{stale.size} entr#{stale.size == 1 ? 'y' : 'ies'} no longer in #{obj.name}"
    mono dropped.map { |e| Display.line(e, key: true) }.join("\n")
  end

  def self.cmd_clear(rest)
    count = Store.items.size
    unless rest =~ /\A(yes|confirm)\z/i
      warn "this would delete all #{count} entries -- run  ;itemdb clear yes  if you mean it"
      return
    end
    backup = Store.backup
    Store.data['items'] = {}
    Store.save
    info "cleared #{count} entries#{" (backup: #{backup})" if backup}"
  end

  def self.cmd_list(rest)
    items = Store.items.values
    items = items.select { |e| e['container'].to_s.downcase.include?(rest.downcase) } unless rest.empty?
    if items.empty?
      info 'nothing recorded yet -- try ;itemdb scan backpack'
      return
    end

    # An item no bard has sung to has nothing worth listing, so it's only counted.
    unsung, items = items.partition { |e| e['status'] == 'no-loresong' }
    waiting = unsung.empty? ? nil : "(#{unsung.size} more #{unsung.size == 1 ? 'needs' : 'need'} " \
                                    'a bard first -- ;itemdb unsung to see them)'
    if items.empty?
      info "nothing readable recorded yet #{waiting}"
      return
    end

    items = items.sort_by { |e| [e['worn_location'].to_s, -e['worth'].to_i] }
    out = ["#{items.size} item#{'s' if items.size != 1} in the db"]
    out.concat(items.map { |e| Display.block(e) })
    out << waiting if waiting
    mono out.join("\n")
  end

  def self.cmd_show(rest)
    if rest.empty?
      warn 'usage: ;itemdb show <name keyword|key>'
      return
    end
    if id_needle?(rest)
      warn_id_needle(rest, 'look items up by name instead')
      return
    end
    found = lookup(rest)
    if found.empty?
      warn "nothing in the db matches '#{rest}'"
    else
      mono found.map { |_key, e| Display.detail(e) }.join("\n\n")
    end
  end

  def self.cmd_search(query)
    found = Query.matches(query)
    if found.empty?
      info "no items match: #{query}"
      return
    end
    items = found.values.sort_by { |e| [e['worn_location'].to_s, -e['worth'].to_i] }
    out = ["#{items.size} item#{'s' if items.size != 1} matching: #{query}"]
    out.concat(items.map { |e| Display.block(e) })

    # What the search found, added up.  Wearing all of it usually isn't possible, which is what
    # the slot table under it is for.  One match has nothing to add up -- its boosts are already
    # on screen -- but the slots it wants are still worth knowing.
    boosts = items.size > 1 ? Display.boost_totals(aggregate_boosts(items)) : []
    unless boosts.empty?
      out << ''
      out << "all #{items.size} of those added together"
      out.concat(boosts)
    end
    # The limits can only come from the game, so read them once if this character has none yet.
    Usage.ensure_captured
    slots = Display.slot_usage(items, 'matched')
    out.concat(['', *slots]) unless slots.empty?
    mono out.join("\n")
  rescue Query::Error => e
    warn e.message
  end

  # ;itemdb usage         read INVENTORY USAGE, record it, show it
  # ;itemdb usage cached  just show what was recorded last time
  def self.cmd_usage(rest)
    Usage.capture unless rest =~ /\A(cached|stored|saved|old)\z/i
    if Usage.rows.empty?
      warn 'no worn slot usage recorded -- run ;itemdb usage in game to read it from INVENTORY USAGE'
      return
    end

    out = ["worn slots for #{XMLData.name} as of #{Usage.captured_at} -- functional only, " \
           'the way INVENTORY USAGE reports them']
    out << '  the limits are your account\'s (a subscription changes them), so they are read ' \
           'from the game rather than assumed'
    out << format('  %4s  %-30s %7s %6s %5s', 'code', 'worn location', 'in use', 'limit', 'free')
    Usage.chart.each do |row|
      out << format('  %4d  %-30s %7s %6s %5s', row[:code], row[:desc],
                    row[:used] || '-', row[:cap] || '-',
                    row[:used] ? row[:cap] - row[:used] : '-')
    end
    # A row the game reports that WORN_SLOTS doesn't list feeds no body area, so it would
    # otherwise vanish out of the summary without a word.
    unknown = Usage.unknown_rows
    unless unknown.empty?
      out << "  #{unknown.size} row#{'s' if unknown.size != 1} this script doesn't know about, " \
             'not counted in the summary below: ' +
             unknown.map { |code, row| "##{code} #{row['desc']}" }.join(', ')
    end
    # Rows are per-slot; the db records a body area, so this is the bridge between the two.
    slots = Display.slot_usage(Store.items.values, 'recorded')
    out.concat(['', *slots]) unless slots.empty?
    mono out.join("\n")
  end

  # Totals across everything in the db.  This is inventory, not a wearable set -- you can't
  # stack two rings on one finger -- so the worn slot table below the boosts is the half that
  # says what of it you could actually put on.
  def self.cmd_totals
    boosts = Display.boost_totals(aggregate_boosts(Store.items.values))
    Usage.ensure_captured
    slots = Display.slot_usage(Store.items.values, 'recorded')
    if boosts.empty? && slots.empty?
      info 'no enhancive boosts recorded yet'
      return
    end

    out = ['db totals -- everything recorded added up, not a wearable set']
    out.concat(['', *boosts]) unless boosts.empty?
    out.concat(['', *slots]) unless slots.empty?
    mono out.join("\n")
  end

  def self.help
    counts = Store.items.size
    mono <<~HELP
      itemdb #{VERSION} -- #{counts} item#{'s' if counts != 1} recorded in #{Store.file}

      ;itemdb scan [container]      recall items not already in the db (default: #{default_container})
      ;itemdb scan [container] quick  ...and don't hold each item to inspect it
      ;itemdb scan [container] retry  ...and re-check items that had no loresong before

      a book or codex is ANALYZEd for its spell specialization (its loresong shows nothing
      useful); that happens even on a quick scan, since analyze is the only place its detail lives

      containers you're wearing and containers in the room both work, so with the locker
      open:  ;itemdb scan bin  /  ;itemdb scan chest  /  ;itemdb scan wardrobe

      ;itemdb refresh [container]   re-recall everything in the container
      ;itemdb refresh <keyword>     re-recall only the items whose name matches
      ;itemdb remove <keyword|key>  delete an entry
      ;itemdb prune [container]     delete entries no longer in that container
      ;itemdb clear                 wipe the db (asks to confirm, backs up first)
      ;itemdb list [container]      one block per item (items awaiting a bard are counted)
      ;itemdb show <keyword|key>    everything recorded about one item
      ;itemdb totals                per stat/skill totals, and worn slots vs your limits
      ;itemdb usage                 read INVENTORY USAGE: your worn slot limits and what's free
                                    (they depend on your subscription, so only the game knows)
      ;itemdb verbose               toggle showing the raw recall text while scanning

      search -- anything that isn't a command above is a query.  Every search ends with the
      boosts of everything it found added up, and the worn slots those items compete for:
        ;itemdb intuition bonus            ;itemdb int>2 and agi bonus=5
        ;itemdb intuition                  ;itemdb int<=10 and crumbly
        ;itemdb int>2                      ;itemdb int and enhancive persist
        ;itemdb stalking                   ;itemdb int and magic persist
        ;itemdb hiding ranks<10            ;itemdb worth>500k and neck
        ;itemdb hiding<5                   ;itemdb flare:lightning or enchant>=10
        ;itemdb neck & (dex | ranged)      ;itemdb not (holy or unholy)
        ;itemdb dex and char:Drafix        ;itemdb sighted or resist:fire>=20

      the db is shared by all your characters; a search spans every one of them unless you
      add char:<name> (also who:<name>) to limit it to the items one character scanned.

      terms:  <name> [base|bonus|ranks] [> >= < <= = !=] <number>
              crumbly, persist, enhancive persist, magic persist, disintegrate, enhancive,
              magical, imbedded, spell:<name>, activator:<verb>, enchant[>N], maxenchant[>N],
              enchanter:<name>, worth>500k,
              flare[:<name>], temp flare  (acid acuity air cold disintegration dispel
                disruption earth fire firewheel grapple "guiding light" lightning magma
                mana plasma steam td terror vacuum water xazkruvrixis),
              charges>50 (either kind), echarges<20, mcharges>100, charges:<phrase>, trained<=50,
              spec, spec:<spell|number>, specranks>=N  (spell-specialization books),
              worn:<slot> (or just neck/finger/...; ear = one ear, 2ear = both),
              container:<name>, char:<name> (or who:<name>),
              material:<name>, name:<word>, holy, unholy, resistant, alterable,
              resist:<type>[>N] (fire cold acid electrical nature crush impact ...),
              padded, weighted, sighted, ensorcelled, sanctified,
              locked | unsung | needs bard (not unlocked yet), unreadable (no response)
      joiners:  "and" (or , or &) and "or" (or |).  negate a term with !term or "not term".
                group with parentheses -- e.g. neck & (dex | ranged) -- with the usual
                precedence: "not" binds tighter than "and", which binds tighter than "or"
    HELP
  end
end

# === entry point ===
ItemDB.main(script.vars[0])
